PMID:39340527,PubMed,39340527,"Effects of probiotics, prebiotics, and synbiotics on cardiometabolic risk factors in children and adolescents with overweight or obesity: a systematic review and Bayesian network meta-analysis.",microbiome,"Moderate to low evidence suggests MSP may be a potential choice for improving BMI and reducing lipids, leptin, and Hs-CRP levels, implying that MSP could aid in managing pediatric obesity and related metabolic issues by modulating the gut microbiota.",,,,,,,,820 participants,detail_hint,supplement_queue,pending,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,Effects of probiotics  prebiotics  and synbiotics on cardiometabolic risk factors in children and adolescents with overweight or obesity  a systematic.pdf,1.000,16,"( 2015) t urkey 77(33/44) Children with obesity 12 Parallel(no) 4 synbiotics: lactobacillus acidophilus 4.3 × 108 CFu/sachet, lactobacillus rhamnosus 4.3 × 108 CFu , Bifidobacterium bifidum 4.3 × 108 CFu , Bifidobacterium longum 4.3 × 108 CFu , enterococcus faecium 8.2 × 108 CFu+fructo-oligosaccharydes, lactulose lifestyle modification t he daily diet of all participants consisted of a decrease of 10% of the daily caloric intake per day. || Our present study systematically reviewed and quantitatively pooled data from 15 RCTs to assess the effects of supplementing probiotics, prebiotics, and synbiotics on obesity indices and cardiomet - abolic risk factors among children and adolescents with overweight or obesity. || Modulating the gut microbiota through supplementation with probiotics, prebiotics, and synbiotics presents a promising approach for managing pediatric obe - sity. || Implications for research Although the association between gut microbiota and obe - sity has been predominantly elucidated in studies involving adults, research on the intervention of probiotics, prebiotics, or synbiotics in childhood obesity remains limited and yields varied outcomes. || (2013) iran 56(nM) Children and adolescents with overweight or obesity 6–18 Parallel(yes) 8 synbiotics: synbiotic capsule: each containing 2 × 108 CFu/d. || Eligible trials included those utiliz - ing any probiotics, prebiotics, or synbiotics for the treatment of overweight or obesity in children and/or adolescents (aged 2–18 years). || In adult popu - lations, with overweight and obesity, both probiotics and synbiotics (combinations of probiotics and prebiotics that confer benefits to the host) have been shown to aid in weight loss (Álvarez-Arraño and Martín-Peláez 2021). || and (5) trials utilizing single-strain probi - otics (SSP), multiple-strains probiotics (MSP), single-ingredient prebiotic (SIP) (including inulin, galactooligosaccharides, fructo-oligosaccharides, and human milk oligosaccharides), compound prebiotics, or any type of synbiotics (SYN).","Our results did not show a significant difference (p = .98, I2 = 35%) (Supplementary 11). || Significant results were only identified between MSP and placebo in WC mea - sure (MD -1.34 cm, 95% CrI [-2.33, -0.35], p = .008, Very low certainty). || Significant results were identified between SYN vs placebo in the LDL-C measure (MD -1.54 mg/dL, 95% CrI [-1.98, -1.09], p < .00001, Very low certainty). || MSP vs P: MD -1.21 mg/L, 95% CrI [-1.45, -0.97], p < .00001) and indirect comparison of SYN vs MSP (MD 0.42 mg/L, 95% CrI [0.13, 0.71]) indicate a significant difference in Hs-CRP . || SYN was the only intervention that significantly reduced the BMI-z score (MD -0.07, 95% CrI [-0.10, -0.04], p < .00001, Low certainty) compared to the placebo ( Table 3(A) , Bottom left). || In the TG network analysis ( Table 3(B), upper right), only significant results were found between MSP and placebo (MD -3.71 mg/dL, 95% CrI [-5.76, -1.67], p = .0004, Low certainty, I 2 = 35%). || Significant improve- ments were observed in MSP vs placebo related to leptin (MD -3.99 ng/mL, 95% CrI [-4.68, -3.3], p < .00001). || SYN signifi - cantly decreased TC levels compared to the Placebo (MD -4.17 mg/dL, 95% CrI [-5.43, -2.89], p < .00001, Low cer - tainty, I2 = 0%).","Physical activity was recommended for all of the children as mild exercise at least 5 times a week for approximately 30–45 min weight, BMi z score, Fat mass, wC Kilic Yildirim et al. || Multiple-strain probiotics (MSP) showed significant efficacy in reducing BMI (Mean Difference (MD) -2.13 kg/m2, 95% credible interval (CrI) [-2.7, -1.57]), waist circumference (MD -1.34 cm, 95% CrI [-2.33, -0.35]), total cholesterol (MD -6.55 mg/dL, 95% CrI [-10.61, -2.45]), triglycerides (MD -3.71 mg/dL, 95% CrI [-5.76, -1.67]), leptin (MD -3.99 ng/mL, 95% CrI [-4.68, -3.3]), and hypersensitive C-reactive protein (Hs-CRP) (MD -1.21 mg/L, 95% CrI [-1.45, -0.97]). || Moreover, MSP also demonstrated a signif - icant decrease in TC compared to the placebo (MD -6.55 mg/dL, 95% CrI [-10.61, -2.45], p = .001, Moderate Certainty, I2 = 0%). || SYN was the only intervention that significantly reduced the BMI-z score (MD -0.07, 95% CrI [-0.10, -0.04], p < .00001, Low certainty) compared to the placebo ( Table 3(A) , Bottom left). || In the analysis of BMI-z score involving 8 studies with 401 participants and 4 comparisons, all results were derived from direct comparisons, and no statistical heterogeneity was observed ( I2 = 0%, p > .5). || presents the mean difference (95% Cri) for BMi-Z (bottom left) and BMi (upper right). || Combining dietary modifications with physical activity inter - ventions only resulted in a slight reduction in BMI-z score (-0.05; || 2017), whereas another study (Liber and Szajewska 2014) reported no effect on BMI-z score or body fat in children with overweight or obe - sity.","Data synthesis and statistical analysis Relevant data were systematically extracted from eligible original randomized controlled trials (RCTs) covering anthropometric measures (BMI, BMI-z score, WC, WHR, WHtR, Weight), metabolic parameters (glucose, insulin, HOMA-IR, TC, TG, HDL, LDL), markers of inflammation (CRP , Hs-CRP , TNF-α, IL-6), and protein hormones (adi - ponectin, leptin). || Outcome measures Primary outcomes The primary outcome measures included BMI-z score, BMI, serum lipid profiles (total cholesterol [TC], triglycerides [TG], high-density lipoprotein cholesterol [HDL-C], and low-density CRITICAL REVIEWS IN FooD SCIENCE AND NuTRITIoN 5289 lipoprotein cholesterol [LDL-C] in mg/dL), homeostatic model assessment of insulin resistance (HOMA-IR), and glucose lev - els (mg/dL). || (4) trials with outcome measure - ments encompassing at least one of the following parameters: weight, BMI, BMI-z score, waist circumference (WC), blood lipid profiles (total cholesterol [TC], triglyceride [TG], low-density lipoprotein cholesterol [LDL-C], and high-density lipoprotein cholesterol [HDL-C]), fasting insulin, fasting glu - cose, homeostatic model assessment of insulin resistance (HOMA-IR), C-reactive protein (CRP) or hypersensitive C-reactive protein (Hs-CRP), tumor necrosis factor-α (TNF-α), interleu || Placebo a ll participants were advised a structured diet and were instructed to engage in 60 min of daily physical activity CrP , Glucose, insulin, t C, t G, Hdl -C, ldl -C sanchis-Chordà et al. || placebo t he lifestyle intervention methods of these two groups were not mentioned in the original text (we exclusively extracted data from participants in the pure probiotic treatment group and the placebo control group.) BMi, BMi z-score, weight, wC, FBG, insulin, C-peptide, HoM a-ir, t G, ldl -C, Hdl -C, t C, leptin, hsCrP Hume, n icolucci, and r eimer (2017) and nicolucci et al. || Multiple-strain probiotics (MSP) showed significant efficacy in reducing BMI (Mean Difference (MD) -2.13 kg/m2, 95% credible interval (CrI) [-2.7, -1.57]), waist circumference (MD -1.34 cm, 95% CrI [-2.33, -0.35]), total cholesterol (MD -6.55 mg/dL, 95% CrI [-10.61, -2.45]), triglycerides (MD -3.71 mg/dL, 95% CrI [-5.76, -1.67]), leptin (MD -3.99 ng/mL, 95% CrI [-4.68, -3.3]), and hypersensitive C-reactive protein (Hs-CRP) (MD -1.21 mg/L, 95% CrI [-1.45, -0.97]). || However, neither MSP nor SYN significantly affected body weight, waist-to-height ratio (WHtR), fasting insulin, HOMA-IR, HDL-C, adiponec - tin, or C-reactive protein (CRP). || weight, BMi z score, wC, wHr, wHtr, FBG, insulin, HoM a-ir, t G, t C, ldl -C, Hdl -C Table 2.","( 2015) t urkey 77(33/44) Children with obesity 12 Parallel(no) 4 synbiotics: lactobacillus acidophilus 4.3 × 108 CFu/sachet, lactobacillus rhamnosus 4.3 × 108 CFu , Bifidobacterium bifidum 4.3 × 108 CFu , Bifidobacterium longum 4.3 × 108 CFu , enterococcus faecium 8.2 × 108 CFu+fructo-oligosaccharydes, lactulose lifestyle modification t he daily diet of all participants consisted of a decrease of 10% of the daily caloric intake per day. || Moreover, MSP also demonstrated a signif - icant decrease in TC compared to the placebo (MD -6.55 mg/dL, 95% CrI [-10.61, -2.45], p = .001, Moderate Certainty, I2 = 0%). || 2019), along with elevated levels of Firmicutes and reduced levels of Bacteroidetes (Bervoets et al. || Additionally, it has been shown to decrease serum TG and IL-6 levels and increase the abundance of fecal Bifidobacterium spp in children and adolescents with obesity (Nicolucci et al. || (2017) iran 64(nM) Children with obesity and naFld 12 Parallel(yes) 12 Multiple-strain probioyics: lactobacillus acidophilus 3 × 109 CFu , Bifidobacterium lactis 6 × 109 CFu , Bifidobacterium bifidum 2 × 109 CFu , lactobacillus rhamnosus 2 × 109 CFu Placebo Healthy lifestyle habits were recommended for participants of both groups; || (2022) China 78(nM) Children with overweight or obesity 6–18 Parallel(yes) 12 Multiple-strain probioyics: w hite kidney bean extract 300 mg, Psyllium husk100 mg, Garcinia cambogia extract100 mg, lactobacillus salivarius aP-32(3 × 109 CFu), lactobacillus rhamnosus bv-77 (3 × 109 CFu), and Bifidobacterium animalis CP-9 (24 × 109 CFu). || (2022) sli lanka 84(nM) Children with obesity and naFld/nasH 5–15 Parallel(yes) 24 Multiple-strain