PMID:40732984,PubMed,40732984,Gut Microbiota Shifts After a Weight Loss Program in Adults with Obesity: The WLM3P Study.,lipid,"The gut microbiota is increasingly recognized as a key modulator in obesity management, influencing host energy balance, lipid metabolism, and inflammatory pathways.",p = 0.03; p < 0.01,,,,,,,58 adults; n = 29,detail_hint,supplement_queue,pending,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,Akkermansia and,,,6 months; 18 months; 3 days,58 adults; n = 29; 58 participants,controlled trial; randomized,diet,p = 0.03; p < 0.01; p < 0.05; p = 0.907; p = 0.897; p = 0.01; p < 0.001; p = 0.02; p = 0.63; p = 0.36,"39.9 kg/m2; increase in alpha diversity (p = 0; change in beta diversity Nutrients 2025, 17, 2360 https://doi; changes were observed in the LCD group; increased levels of Faecalibacterium; reduced microbial diversity and depletion of beneficial short-chain fatty acid (SCFA)-producing; improved inflammatory profiles during weight loss interventions [5,7–10]; improved energy efficiency and metabolic health, including Akkermansia and Christensenellaceae, a; increase Faecalibacterium and Bifidobacterium, improving metabolic and inflammatory markers [15,16; reduce microbial diversity [17,18]; increase proteolytic bacteria such as Bacteroides [9] but may re- duce beneficial SCFA producers i; improvements [4–6]; changes, isolating the initial phase of weight loss provides a unique window to capture early mi; changes, and correlations with body composition and lipid markers; changes that correlate with improvements in fat mass and visceral adiposity, we aim to provide a; improve metabolic health; 40 kg/m; 70%","Gut Microbiota Shifts After a Weight Loss Program in Adults with Obesity: The WLM3P Study. || Article Gut Microbiota Shifts After a Weight Loss Program in Adults with Obesity: The WLM3P Study Vanessa Pereira 1,2, *,†, Amanda Cuevas-Sierra 1,3, *,† , Victor de la O 3,4 , Rita Salvado 1,5 , Inês Barreiros-Mota 1,6 , Inês Castela 1,6 , Alexandra Camelo 7,8 , Inês Brandão 7,8 , Christophe Espírito Santo 7,8 , Ana Faria 1,6,9 , Conceição Calhau 1,9 , Marta P . || Objective: This study investigated the effects of the multicomponent Weight Loss Maintenance 3 Phases Program (WLM3P), which integrates caloric restriction, a high-protein low-carbohydrate diet, time-restricted eating (10h TRE), dietary supplemen- tation (prebiotics and phytochemicals), and digital app-based support on gut microbiota composition compared to a standard low-carbo || The analysis focused exclusively on the 6-month weight loss period corresponding to Phases 1 and 2 of the WLM3P intervention. || Methods: In this sub-analysis of a random- ized controlled trial (ClinicalTrials.gov Identifier: NCT04192357), 58 adults with obesity (BMI 30.0–39.9 kg/m2) were randomized to the WLM3P (n = 29) or LCD (n = 29) groups. || Notably, higher Faecalibacterium abundance was associated with greater reductions in fat mass (kg, %) and visceral adiposity (cm2) in the WLM3P group compared to LCD (p < 0.01). || Conclusions: These findings suggest a potential microbiota-mediated mechanism in weight loss, where Faecalibacterium may enhance fat reduction effectiveness in the context of the WLM3P intervention.","Academic Editor: Jose M. Miranda Received: 27 May 2025 Revised: 2 July 2025 Accepted: 7 July 2025 Published: 18 July 2025 Citation: Pereira, V .; Cuevas-Sierra, A.; de la O, V .; Salvado, R.; Barreiros-Mota, I.; Castela, I.; Camelo, A.; Brandão, I.; Santo, C.E.; Faria, A.; et al. Gut Microbiota Shifts After a Weight Loss Program in Adults with Obesity: The WLM3P Study. Nutrients 2025, 17, 2360. https://doi.org/ 10.3390/nu17142360 Copyright: © 2025 by the authors. Licensee MDPI, Basel, Switzerland. This article is an open access article distributed under the terms and conditions of the Creative Commons Attribution (CC BY) license (https://creativecommons.org/ licenses/by/4.0/). Article Gut Mi",ok,,nutrients-17-02360.pdf,1.000,17,"These results suggest that the interaction between Faecalibacterium and the intervention ( p < 0.001) was more pronounced in the WLM3P group, highlighting the potential role of this microbial genus in mediating the effects of diet on body composition in the context of the WLM3P intervention. || Dietary Interventions To induce WL, a personalized dietary plan was prescribed for each participant, aiming for 70% of their daily energy requirements (DER) in both dietary interventions (baseline to month 6). || To address these gaps, the present study investigates gut microbiota dynamics specif- ically during the 6-month active weight loss phase of the Weight Loss Maintenance 3 Phases program (WLM3P) [22], a multicomponent intervention combining caloric restric- tion, a high-protein, low-carbohydrate diet, 10-h TRE, targeted supplementation, and app-based behavioural support. || WLM3P (Intervention Group) The WLM3P is a structured nutritional and behavioral program based on seven components: Dietary intervention with 3 Phases (Phase 1 and Phase 2—weight loss period, and Phase 3—weight maintenance period), regular one-to-one consultations, behavioral strategies, time-restricted eating (10-h TRE), dietary supplements, high-protein specific food, and a mobile and web application. || Beta diversity was significantly different in WLP3P group after 6 months of intervention ( p < 0.01). || The Shannon Index (Figure 1a) was calculated and revealed significantly higher values for the WLM3P group after 6 months of intervention ( p = 0.03). || All parameters showed significant time- by-intervention interactions (p < 0.001), indicating a more pronounced effect of WLM3P over the 6-month period. || Results: After 6-month, the WLM3P group showed a significant increase in alpha diversity (p = 0.03) and a significant change in beta diversity Nutrients 2025, 17, 2360 https://doi.org/10.3390/nu17142360 Nutrients 2025, 17, 2360 2 of 17 (p < 0.01), while no significant changes were observed in the LCD group.","Results: After 6-month, the WLM3P group showed a significant increase in alpha diversity (p = 0.03) and a significant change in beta diversity Nutrients 2025, 17, 2360 https://doi.org/10.3390/nu17142360 Nutrients 2025, 17, 2360 2 of 17 (p < 0.01), while no significant changes were observed in the LCD group. || 30.0% male), with no significant differences in age (p = 0.907) or gender distribution (p = 0.897). || However, no significant results were found in alpha diversity after 6 months in LCD group (p = 0.63). || No statistically significant differences in microbial diversity within LCD group at the end of study (p = 0.36). || The between-group differences in muscle mass reduction, LDL-cholesterol, HDL-cholesterol, triglycerides and Tg/HDL ratio were not statistically significant (Table 1). || Christensenellaceae R-7 also increased significantly in WLM3P , a taxon robustly associ- ated with leanness, lower visceral fat, and improved metabolic profiles [11]. || Analysis of the Differential Microbiota Abundances at Genus Level Between WLM3P and LCD Groups After 6 Months of Intervention Tables 2 and 3 present the bacterial genus that presented a significant difference in relative abundance for WLM3P group (Table 2) and LCD (Table 3) after 6 months, com- pared by EdgeR. || Beta diversity was significantly different in WLP3P group after 6 months of intervention ( p < 0.01).","Compared to a standard low-carbohydrate diet (LCD), the WLM3P group achieved significantly greater reductions in body weight, BMI, fat mass, visceral fat, and waist circumference, with no significant differences in muscle mass loss or blood lipid profiles. || Romboutsia genus showed significant positive corre- lations with BMI, fat mass (Kg), fat mass percentage, visceral fat and waist-hip ratio at 6 months in LCD group. || Notably, higher Faecalibacterium abundance was associated with greater reductions in fat mass (kg, %) and visceral adiposity (cm2) in the WLM3P group compared to LCD (p < 0.01). || Body mass index (BMI) and waist circumference (WC) were measured according to the Directorate-General of Health’s guidelines [25]. || Body mass index (BMI) was calculated as the ratio between body weight and squared height (kg/m 2) and the BMI classification criteria was following according to the World Health Organization [24]. || Furthermore, while the predictive interaction between Faecalibacterium and total body weight loss (kg) and BMI reduction was also tested, these interactions were only marginally significant, with p-values of 0.10 and 0.08, respectively. || As shown in Figure 4, the abundance of Faecalibacterium at 6 months was signif- icantly associated with reductions in fat mass (kg) (Figure 4a), fat mass percentage (Figure 4b), and visceral fat (Figure 4c), adjusted by age, sex and BMI. || p = 0.01), BMI (∆ = -2.61 kg/m2, 95% CI: -3.69 to -1.52;","The between-group differences in muscle mass reduction, LDL-cholesterol, HDL-cholesterol, triglycerides and Tg/HDL ratio were not statistically significant (Table 1). || Phascolarctobacterium showed a significant positive correlation with LDL cholesterol, triglycerides and tg/HDL ratio. || The following biochemical markers were assessed in the blood samples, assessing high-density lipoprotein cholesterol (HDL-c), triglycerides, and low-density lipoprotein cholesterol (LDL-c), following the instructions provided by the manufacturers. || 3.70) HDL cholesterol (mg/dL) Baseline 47.9 (43.6; || -0.1) LDL cholesterol (mg/dL) Baseline 114.0 (101.6; || Results: After 6-month, the WLM3P group showed a significant increase in alpha diversity (p = 0.03) and a significant change in beta diversity Nutrients 2025, 17, 2360 https://doi.org/10.3390/nu17142360 Nutrients 2025, 17, 2360 2 of 17 (p < 0.01), while no significant changes were observed in the LCD group. || 16.9) Tg/HDL ratio Baseline 3.0 (2.3; || 2.9) Triglycerides (mg/dL) Baseline 129.1 (106.2;","Results: After 6-month, the WLM3P group showed a significant increase in alpha diversity (p = 0.03) and a significant change in beta diversity Nutrients 2025, 17, 2360 https://doi.org/10.3390/nu17142360 Nutrients 2025, 17, 2360 2 of 17 (p < 0.01), while no significant changes were observed in the LCD group. || High-fiber diets increase Faecalibacterium and Bifidobacterium, improving metabolic and inflammatory markers [15,16]. || Diverse dietary interventions can reshape the gut microbiota, often enhancing SCFA- producing taxa while reducing pro-inflammatory genera [ 4–6]. || High-protein diets can increase proteolytic bacteria such as Bacteroides [9] but may re- duce beneficial SCFA producers if fiber intake is low [ 19]. || Conclusions: These findings suggest a potential microbiota-mediated mechanism in weight loss, where Faecalibacterium may enhance fat reduction effectiveness in the context of the WLM3P intervention. || Notably, the enrichment of SCFA-producing taxa such as Faecalibacterium and Christensenellaceae R-7 highlights a potentially important microbiota-mediated component of the intervention’s metabolic benefits. || Blue colour represents the microbiota at baseline and red corresponds to microbiota composition after 6 months, providing a clear visual representation of the taxa that are significantly enriched or depleted in each group, by time. || Obesity-related dysbiosis typically involves reduced microbial diversity and depletion of beneficial short-chain fatty acid (SCFA)-producing taxa such as Faecalibacterium, Christensenellaceae, and Akkermansia, contributing to metabolic dysfunction and impaired gut barrier integrity [4–7].","Results: After 6-month, the WLM3P group showed a significant increase in alpha diversity (p = 0.03) and a significant change in beta diversity Nutrients 2025, 17, 2360 https://doi.org/10.3390/nu17142360 Nutrients 2025, 17, 2360 2 of 17 (p < 0.01), while no significant changes were observed in the LCD group. || p = 0.01), BMI (∆ = -2.61 kg/m2, 95% CI: -3.69 to -1.52; || p < 0.001), fat mass (∆ = -6.09 kg, 95% CI: -9.57 to -2.61; || p < 0.001), fat mass percentage ( ∆ = -5.06%, 95% CI: -8.70 to -1.42; || p < 0.001) and waist-to-hip ratio (∆ = -0.04, 95% CI: -0.07 to -0.01;","baseline and 6 || increase in alpha diversity (p = 0.03 || change in beta diversity Nutrients 2025 || reduction effectiveness in the context of the WLM3 || increase Faecalibacterium and Bifidobacterium, improving metabolic and inflammatory markers [15 || increase proteolytic bacteria such as Bacteroides [9 || baseline to month 6 || baseline and after six months by a trained nutritionist using bioelectrical impedance analysis (Inbody® model 770 || mean differences with 95% || Baseline Data and Effect of the WLMP3 || Baseline demographic characteristics were comparable between the WLM3 || mean age: 43 || mean age: 47 || reduction, LDL-cholesterol, HDL-cholesterol, triglycerides and Tg/HDL ratio were not statistically significant (Table 1 || Baseline 97.1 || Baseline 34.1 || Baseline 41.5 || Baseline 43.3","Academic Editor: Jose M. Miranda Received: 27 May 2025 Revised: 2 July 2025 Accepted: 7 July 2025 Published: 18 July 2025 Citation: Pereira, V .; Cuevas-Sierra, A.; de la O, V .; Salvado, R.; Barreiros-Mota, I.; Castela, I.; Camelo, A.; Brandão, I.; Santo, C.E.; Faria, A.; et al. Gut Microbiota Shifts After a Weight Loss Program in Adults with Obesity: The WLM3P Study. Nutrients 2025, 17, 2360. https://doi.org/ 10.33",diet || intervention || control || Akkermansia || Placebo || Prebiotic || Probiotic || Synbiotic,7 g || 3011 g,6 months || 18 months || 3 days,58 adults || n = 29 || 58 participants,ok,
