PMID:42044842,PubMed,42044842,"The effect of a two-week low glycaemic index, higher fibre diet versus high glycaemic index diet on body composition, ectopic lipids, inflammatory biomarkers, gastrointestinal hormones and gut microbiota in metabolic dysfunction-associated steatotic liver disease (MASLD): A pilot randomized cross-over study.",weight,"Additionally, the LGI diet resulted in significant reductions in body weight (-1.2 kg, p = 0.018) and body mass index (-0.38 kg/m2, p = 0.017), as well as increased pre-meal appetite scores during the middle of the diet period and decreased interleukin 6; IL-6 (-0.9 pg/mL) and glucagon (-13.7 pg/mL) levels (all p < 0.05).",p = 0.043; p = 0.018; p = 0.017; p < 0.05,,,,,,,7 participants,detail_hint,expansion_queue,pending,,PIIS240545772600402X.pdf,1.000,10,,,,7 participants; 269 participants,microbiota; glucose; HOMA-IR; body weight; body mass index; insulin; BMI,"Original article The effect of a two-w eek low glycaemic index, higher fibre diet versus high glycaemic index diet on body composition, ectopic lipids, inflammatory biomarkers, gastrointestinal hormones and gut microbiota in metabolic dysfunction-associated steatotic liver disease (MASLD): A pilot randomized cross-over study Amina A. Al-Aw adi a,b,c,d,1 , Jane I. Grove a,b , Stephen Bawden b,e,f,g , Amrita Vijay b,h , Ana M. Valdes b,h , Penny Gow land b,e , Moir a A. Taylor b,i,2 , Guruprasad P. Aithal a,b,*,2 a Nottingham Digestive Diseases Centre, Translational Medical Sciences, School of Medicine, University of Nottingham, Nottingham, UK b National Institute for Health Research (NIHR) Nottingham Biomedical Research Centre, Nottingham University Hospitals NHS Trust and the University of Nottingham, Nottingham, UK c Al-Sabah Hospital, Ministry of Health, Civil Service Commission, Kuwai",Bifidobacterium and; Lactobacillus levels; Lactobacillus and,,,2 weeks; 12 months; 1 week; 24 weeks,7 participants; 269 participants; 8 participants; n = 7; n = 13; n = 154; n = 71; n = 98; n = 15; n = 5,pilot; randomized; randomised; crossover; controlled trial; clinical trial,diet,p = 0.043; p = 0.018; p = 0.017; p < 0.05; p > 0.05; p < 0.0001; p = 0.0001; p = 0.4; p = 0.51; p = 0.59; p = 0.66; p = 0.63; p = 0.028; p = 0.61; p = 0.23; p = 0.09,"reduce liver fat, blood glucose, and gut microbiota imbalance arresting MASLD progress; reduce liver fat more than a high GI (HGI) diet, despite matched energy (isoenergetic); reduced following the LGI diet (8; 57%; changes in blood glycaemic biomarkers, including a significant reduction in HOMA-IR (␀; 1.2 kg; 0.38 kg/; increased pre-meal appetite scores during the middle of the diet period and decreased in; reduce liver fat in patients with MASLD and may be an important factor in preventative; increased globally, leading to significant risks of metabolic dysfunction, liver disease; increase from 18th in 1990 [2]; 5%; 38%; increase in hepatic stores of fat when compared with isoenergetic low GI (LGI) diet cons; reduce circulating glucose spikes, insulin resistance, hepatic glucose influx, and de; reduced liver fat compared with baseline, but confounding factors, such as other lifes; changes[15–17]; 25 kg/m","The impact of the LGI diet on liver fat content, measured using Magnetic Resonance Spectroscopy ( 1 H-MRS) liver-related blood biomarkers, appetite (using subjective visual analogue scales) and gut microbiome composition w as identified. || Relative liver fat fraction w as mar kedly reduced following the LGI diet (8.57%).Observ ations in the LGI group coincided with changes in blood glycaemic biomarkers, including a significant reduction in HOMA-IR (␀ 1.78, p = 0.043). || Additionally, the LGI diet resulted in significant reductions in body weight (␀ 1.2 kg, p = 0.018) and body mass index (␀ 0.38 kg/ m 2 , p = 0.017), as well as increased pre-meal appetite scores during the middle of the diet period and decreased interleukin 6; || Patients with MASLD often consume high GI (HGI) foods, which are associated with insulin resistance, inflammation, metabolic dysfunction, and liver damag e [7,9]. || LGI diets have been proposed as an effective lifestyle intervention in MASLD due to their potential ability to reduce circulating glucose spikes, insulin resistance, hepatic glucose influx, and de novo lipogenesis [11]. || High-fibre diets, how ever, are not always associated with LGI, as some fibre types can have a lesser impact on postprandial glycemia and hyperinsulinemia [14].","Original article The effect of a two-w eek low glycaemic index, higher fibre diet versus high glycaemic index diet on body composition, ectopic lipids, inflammatory biomarkers, gastrointestinal hormones and gut microbiota in metabolic dysfunction-associated steatotic liver disease (MASLD): A pilot randomized cross-over study Amina A. Al-Aw adi a,b,c,d,1 , Jane I. Grove a,b , Stephen Bawden b,e,f,g , Amrita Vijay b,h , Ana M. Valdes b,h , Penny Gow land b,e , Moir a A. Taylor b,i,2 , Guruprasad P. Aithal a,b,*,2 a Nottingham Digestive Diseases Centre, Translational Medical Sciences, School of Medicine, University of Nottingham, Nottingham, UK b National Institute for Health Research (NIHR) No",ok,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,PIIS240545772600402X.pdf,1.000,10,"Within-group compari- sons show ed a significant reduction in HOMA-IR after LGI diet (p = 0.043). || A significant difference in beta-diversity change w as found between the two diets (LGI = ␀ 0.02 vs HGI = +0.0001, p = 0.014). || For inflammatory markers, a significant decrease in IL-6 w as observed following the LGI diet (␀ 0.9 pg/mL, p = 0.028, r = 0.45). || Both diets resulted in a slight increase in the ATP forward rate, though the changes were not statistically significant (HGI = 0.3, p = 0.53; || Although a greater numerical reduction in IL-6 levels w as seen after the HGI diet (␀ 1.13 pg/mL), this w as not statistically signifi- cant (p = 0.61). || Both diets resulted in a slight reduction in alpha- diversity, with a significant decrease observed after the HGI diet (␀ 0.26, p = 0.03) (Appendix S2 Table S5). || Absolute level of liver fat w as reduced more after LGI diet (␀ 1.56 ± 3.16%) compared to the HGI diet (␀ 1.03 ± 5.09%), although this difference w as not statistically significant (p = 0.4, r = -0.26). || For gut hormones, no significant change w as observed after the HGI (␀ 4.6 pg/mL, p = 0.23), while a significant reduction in glucagon levels w as found following the LGI diet (␀ 13.72 pg/mL, p = 0.028).","A significant difference in beta-diversity change w as found between the two diets (LGI = ␀ 0.02 vs HGI = +0.0001, p = 0.014). || Both diets resulted in a slight increase in the ATP forward rate, though the changes were not statistically significant (HGI = 0.3, p = 0.53; || Absolute level of liver fat w as reduced more after LGI diet (␀ 1.56 ± 3.16%) compared to the HGI diet (␀ 1.03 ± 5.09%), although this difference w as not statistically significant (p = 0.4, r = -0.26). || Relative liver fat fraction w as mar kedly reduced following the LGI diet (8.57%).Observ ations in the LGI group coincided with changes in blood glycaemic biomarkers, including a significant reduction in HOMA-IR (␀ 1.78, p = 0.043). || Additionally, the LGI diet resulted in significant reductions in body weight (␀ 1.2 kg, p = 0.018) and body mass index (␀ 0.38 kg/ m 2 , p = 0.017), as well as increased pre-meal appetite scores during the middle of the diet period and decreased interleukin 6; || A significant difference between the two diets w as observed in changes in body weight (p = 0.018, r = -0.89) and BMI (p = 0.043, r = 0.90), with significant reductions following the LGI diet only (Body weight: ␀ 1.2 ± 1.06 kg, and BMI: ␀ 0.38 ± 0.35, both p < 0.05). || Both diets resulted in a slight reduction in alpha- diversity, with a significant decrease observed after the HGI diet (␀ 0.26, p = 0.03) (Appendix S2 Table S5). || Acetic acid significantly decreased after HGI (p = 0.028) and show ed a non- significant increase after LGI (p = 0.31), also yielding a large ef- fect (r = 0.70).","A significant difference between the two diets w as observed in changes in body weight (p = 0.018, r = -0.89) and BMI (p = 0.043, r = 0.90), with significant reductions following the LGI diet only (Body weight: ␀ 1.2 ± 1.06 kg, and BMI: ␀ 0.38 ± 0.35, both p < 0.05). || Additionally, the LGI diet resulted in significant reductions in body weight (␀ 1.2 kg, p = 0.018) and body mass index (␀ 0.38 kg/ m 2 , p = 0.017), as well as increased pre-meal appetite scores during the middle of the diet period and decreased interleukin 6; || Additionally, studies indicate that liver fat reduction can occur without significant changes in body weight or BMI in pa- tients with MASLD or metabolic syndrome [45–47]. || Fractional change from baseline show ed a significant mean reduction in body weight of 1.22% (95%CI: ␀ 2.19% to ␀ 0.26%) and BMI of 1.17% (95%CI: ␀ 2.07% to ␀ 0.27%) following the LGI diet, compared to the HGI diet, as illustrated in Fig. || Within-group compari- sons show ed a significant reduction in HOMA-IR after LGI diet (p = 0.043). || A significant difference in beta-diversity change w as found between the two diets (LGI = ␀ 0.02 vs HGI = +0.0001, p = 0.014). || For inflammatory markers, a significant decrease in IL-6 w as observed following the LGI diet (␀ 0.9 pg/mL, p = 0.028, r = 0.45). || Both diets resulted in a slight increase in the ATP forward rate, though the changes were not statistically significant (HGI = 0.3, p = 0.53;","Within-group compari- sons show ed a significant reduction in HOMA-IR after LGI diet (p = 0.043). || Relative liver fat fraction w as mar kedly reduced following the LGI diet (8.57%).Observ ations in the LGI group coincided with changes in blood glycaemic biomarkers, including a significant reduction in HOMA-IR (␀ 1.78, p = 0.043). || How ever, changes in gly- caemic biomarkers show ed greater reductions following the LGI diet compare d to the HGI diet in fasting glucose (␀ 0.84 vs ␀ 0.35 mmol/L, r = 0.45), insulin (␀ 2.94 vs + 0.3 mIU/L, r = 0.51) and HOMA-IR (␀ 1.78 vs ␀ 0.29, r = 0.51). || A significant difference in beta-diversity change w as found between the two diets (LGI = ␀ 0.02 vs HGI = +0.0001, p = 0.014). || For inflammatory markers, a significant decrease in IL-6 w as observed following the LGI diet (␀ 0.9 pg/mL, p = 0.028, r = 0.45). || Both diets resulted in a slight increase in the ATP forward rate, though the changes were not statistically significant (HGI = 0.3, p = 0.53; || Although a greater numerical reduction in IL-6 levels w as seen after the HGI diet (␀ 1.13 pg/mL), this w as not statistically signifi- cant (p = 0.61). || Both diets resulted in a slight reduction in alpha- diversity, with a significant decrease observed after the HGI diet (␀ 0.26, p = 0.03) (Appendix S2 Table S5).","A meta - analysis of 64 RCTs on healthy subjects also confirmed that di- etary fibre interventions specifically increase Bifidobacterium and Lactobacillus levels without affecting α-diversity [53] similar to the present study. || Within-group compari- sons show ed a significant reduction in HOMA-IR after LGI diet (p = 0.043). || A significant difference in beta-diversity change w as found between the two diets (LGI = ␀ 0.02 vs HGI = +0.0001, p = 0.014). || For inflammatory markers, a significant decrease in IL-6 w as observed following the LGI diet (␀ 0.9 pg/mL, p = 0.028, r = 0.45). || Both diets resulted in a slight increase in the ATP forward rate, though the changes were not statistically significant (HGI = 0.3, p = 0.53; || Although a greater numerical reduction in IL-6 levels w as seen after the HGI diet (␀ 1.13 pg/mL), this w as not statistically signifi- cant (p = 0.61). || Both diets resulted in a slight reduction in alpha- diversity, with a significant decrease observed after the HGI diet (␀ 0.26, p = 0.03) (Appendix S2 Table S5). || Acetic acid significantly decreased after HGI (p = 0.028) and show ed a non- significan