erved between the study gr oups in ter ms of baseline charact eristics including anthropometric v ariables, demogra phic information, ph ysical ac - tivity , metabolic v ariables, and depression or appetite scores ( P > 0.05). || How ev er , based on the post hoc analy sis, the between-gr oup analy ses indicated that the decrease in W C ( P = 0.0 03), WHtR ( P = 0.0 04), body fat percenta ge ( P = 0.032), AST ( P = 0.04 5), and depression ( P = 0.04 7) in the AMY gr oup w as significantly more than the other groups. || Results : In the AMY group, significant re ductions w ere observed in w aist circumfer ence (P = 0.0 03), w aist-to-heig ht ra tio (P = 0.0 04), body fat percentag e (P = 0.032), aspartate aminot ransfer ase levels (P = 0.045), and appetite scores (P = 0.04 7) compared with the control group. || Moreo ver , the changes in the outcomes of inter est within the LR Y group wer e not statistically significant when compared to the other groups ( T able 4 ). || Variables AMY Group, (n = 22) LRY Group, (n = 21) Control Group, (n = 22) Intervention Effect Change Intragroup P * Change Intragroup P * Change Intragroup P * P ¥ Weight (kg) ␀ 1.69 (1.92) < 0.001 ␀ 1.41 (2.20) 0.008 ␀ 0.63 (1.79) 0.114 0.191 Body mass index (kg/m2) ␀ 0.63 (0.74) 0.001 ␀ 0.52 (0.81) 0.008 ␀ 0.22 (0.64) 0.114 0.171 Waist circumference (cm) ␀ 4.27 (4.00) < 0.001 ␀ 1.61 (2.13) 0.002 ␀ 1.27 (2.67) 0.037 0.003 Hip circumference (cm) ␀ 1.63 (2.01) 0.001 ␀ 1.33 (1.74) 0.002 ␀ 0.68 (1.78) 0.087 0.226 WHR || In the control group or LFY group, a within gr oup decreasin g impact w as detect ed in W C ( P = 0.03 7), WHtR ( P = 0.033), TC ( P = 0.0 03), and LDL ( P = 0.04 4) ( T able 4 ).","The main results rev ealed a statistically significant decrease within the AMY group in v arious paramet ers, including wei ght ( P < 0.0 0 1), body mass inde x (BMI) ( P = 0.0 0 1), w aist circumfer - ence (W C) ( P < 0.0 0 1), hip circumfer ence (HC) ( P = 0.0 0 1), w aist- to-hip ratio (WHR) ( P = 0.04 7), 