n ¼ 18) 7 (39%) 11 (61%) Number of species /C21 2( n ¼ 15) 4 (27%) 11 (73%) || The candidate mechanisms to explain the potential role of the gut dysbiosis in the etiology of obesity include increased energy har- vesting by the gut microbiota, increased fat storage in the adipose tissue, reduced secretion of anorexigenic hormones, and low-grade inﬂammation induced by increased intestinal permeability ( Fig. || [58] Meijnikman AS, Gerdes VE, Nieuwdorp M, Herrema H.","The outcomes evaluated in the 33 studies included body weight (n ¼ 28), BMI (n ¼ 29), waist circumference (n ¼ 21), waist-to-hip ratio (n ¼ 12), waist-to-height ratio (n ¼ 2), total fat mass (kg) (n ¼ 17), total body fat (%) (n ¼ 15), abdominal fat area (n ¼ 5), subcutaneous fat area (n ¼ 5) and visceral fat area (n ¼ 8). || As main results we observed that ~30% of the RCTs reported a signi ﬁcant decrease in body weight and body mass index (BMI) and ~50% found a signi ﬁcant reduction in waist circumference and total fat mass. || Clinical Nutrition 42 (2023) 835 e847 842 beneﬁcial results of probiotics when the interventions were char- acterized by /C21 12 weeks duration, probiotics dose /C21 1010 CFU/day, in capsules, sachets or powder, and without concomitant energy restriction, and the outcome measures included assessment of body adiposity deposits by evaluating body fat mass and waist circumference, instead of body weight and BMI. || The evidence of probiotics effects on body adiposity may improve and be more consistent in future RCTs which include methodological characteristics such as longer duration, higher dose, non-dairy vehicle, non-concurrent energy restriction and use of more accurate measures of body fat deposits (e.g., body fat mass and waist circumference) instead of body weight and BMI. || We observed that only around one-third of the RCTs that evaluated body weight and BMI reported a signiﬁcant decrease in these parameters, while 50% of RCTs that evaluated waist circumference and total fat