. || Effect of alive probiotic on insulin resistance in type 2 diabetes patients: randomized clinical trial. || Cultural and environmental factors are main contributors to the rapid recent increase in the prevalence of obesity [ 2]. || Conversely, most of the studies evaluating fat mass and abdominal fat area observed a signiﬁcative reduction in these variables with probiotics supple- mentation (53% and 60%, respectively). || Systematic reviews with meta-analysis evaluating this issue observed signi ﬁcant decrease of body weight, anthropometric pa- rameters and/or body fat after probiotic supplementation [ 21e23]. || As main results we observed that ~30% of the RCTs reported a signi ﬁcant decrease in body weight and body mass index (BMI) and ~50% found a signi ﬁcant reduction in waist circumference and total fat mass.","A recent systematic review, including 32 studies, observed that individuals with obesity have a greater Firmicutes/Bacteroidetes ratio, higher counts of Firmicutes, Fusobacteria, Proteobacteria, Mollicutes, Lactobacillus (reuteri), and less Verrucomicrobia ( Akkermansia muciniphila), Faecalibacterium (prausnitzii), Bacteroidetes, Meth- anobrevibacter smithii, Lactobacillus plantarum and paracasei [56]. || In healthy adults, the gut microbiota composition is character- ized by its abundance, diversity and richness, and is predominantly composed of two main phyla, Bacteroidetes and Firmicutes, which corresponds to approximately 90% of the gut microbiota [ 28,30]. || Effect of probiotic Lactobacillus plantarum Dad-13 powder consumption on the gut microbiota and intestinal health of overweight adults. || Association of microbiota with obesity Many studies have identi ﬁed quantitative and qualitative shifts in the microbiota composition of individuals with obesity compared with their lean counterparts, including a decrease in microbial diversity [ 47e53]. || The gut microbiota contains glycosidases and lyases that are essential for the degradation of polysaccharides, food components indigestible by humans, as well as their fermentation into absorb- able monosaccharides and (SCFAs) such as propionate, butyrate and acetate [ 67e70]. || For example, a high-fat diet and a western dietary pattern (characterized by a high intake of processed foods, saturated fats and sugar), have been associated with an unfavorable microbiome, while a higher intake of ﬁber and adherence to a health dietary pattern, like the Mediterranean diet, have been associated with bene ﬁcial effects in gut microbiota [31,61e66]. || Some of the proposed mechanisms for the bene ﬁcial effects of probiotics on host health include colonization, competitive exclu- sion of pathogens, improvement in the gut microbiota composition, synthesis of enzymes and short chain fatty acids (SCFAs), mucin production, reduction of intestinal permeability (improving barrier function), modulation of the immune system and interaction with the brain-gut axis [ 39e42]. || Microbiota and SCFA in lean and overweight healthy subjects.","Regarding safety and adverse effects, it is worth mentioning that 22 out of the 33 RCTs assessed the occurrence of adverse events. || 95% CI: /C0 0.30, /C0 0.13, p < 0.001), and waist circumference (/C0 0.60 cm; || P < 0.0001) and body adiposity in individuals with overweight and obesity [25], but the results of the different studies were controversial. || Cultural and environmental factors are main contributors to the rapid recent increase in the prevalence of obesity [ 2]. || In gut dysbiosis, suppression of the AMPK occurs, leading to less oxidation of fatty acids and an increase in triglyceride synthesis [67,71].","decrease in body weight and body mass index (BMI) and ~50% || increase of 30% || increase in the prevalence of obesity [ 2 || decrease of body weight, anthropometric pa- rameters and/or body fat after probiotic supplementation [ 21 || reduction in body weight ( /C0 || decrease in microbial diversity [ 47 || increase in triglyceride