on after diet and probiotics in overweight breast cancer survivors: a randomized open-label pilot intervention trial Marianna Pellegrini M.D. a, Mirko Ippolito M.D. a, Taira Monge R.D. a, Rossella Violi R.D. a, Paola Cappello M.D. b, Ilario Ferrocino Ph.D. c, Luca Simone Cocolin Ph.D. c, Antonella De Francesco M.D. a, Simona Bo Ph.D. d, Concetta Finocchiaro Ph.D. a,* a Department of Clinical Nutrition, Citt /C18a della Salute e della Scienza, Turin, Italy b CeRMS Labortory of Tumor Immunology, University of Turin, Italy c Department of Agricultural, Forest and Food Sciences, University of Turin, Italy d Department of Medical Science, ",ok,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,Gut microbiota composition after diet and probiotics in overweight breast cancer survivors_ a randomized open-label pilot interv(科研通-ablesci.com).pdf,1.000,9,"Moreover, the a diversity indicated that there were no differences, in terms of complexity (P > 0.05), between the dietary intervention (control versus probi- otic) at baseline as well as across time. || (Bologna, Italy) provided the probiotic product, each sachet containing 4 £ 109 colony-forming units (CFU) of B. || Intervention Thirty-four female patients were randomly allocated to MD for 4 mo plus 1 sachet/d of probiotics for the ﬁrst 2 mo of the study (intervention group, n = 16) or MD alone for 4 mo (control group, n = 18). || Dietary supplementation with probiotics, such as bacterial strains exerting bene ﬁcial effects on their host, regulates the gut microbiota structure and function through the interaction with the commensal bacteria and the expression of microbial enzymes [28]. || Methods: A total of 34 BC survivors were randomly assigned to an MD for 4 mo plus 1 sachet/d of probiotics (Biﬁdobacterium longum BB536, Lactobacillus rhamnosus HN001) for the ﬁrst 2 mo (intervention group, n = 16) or an MD alone for 4 mo (control group, n = 18). || An agrarian diet lead to an increase inPrevotella,whereas diets rich in proteins and fats lead to an increase in Bacteroidesand Clostri- diales [46/C048].H e r ew ef o u n dar e d u c t i o ni nBacteroidesin probiotic- treated participants, probably because of a reduction in protein and lipid intakes from T0 to T2. || We then found in probiotic-treated patients an increase in Butyricicoccus often associated with the low-fat diets [51],w h i c h could be beneﬁcial because of its ability to reduce the incidence and severity of in ﬂammation or insulin sensitivity [52].I ts h o u l db e emphasized that from the correlation analysis we have discovered an inverse relationship betweenLachnospiraand the CRP value: Lachno- spira’s h a v eap r o t e c t i v er o l ei ni nﬂammatory condition[53]. || Probiotics, prebiotics, synbiotics and insulin sen- sitivity.","The Bacteroidetes-to-Firmicutes ratio signi ﬁcantly decreased in the intervention group and increased in controls ( P = 0.004). || Results: After 2 mo of probiotic administration the number of bacterial species ( P = 0.01) and the bacterial diversity assessed with the Chao1 index ( P = 0.004) signi ﬁcantly increased; || 3 ) indicated statistically signi ﬁcant differences in several taxa (P < 0.05) between T0 and T2. || in the intervention group waist circumference ( P = 0.012), waist-to-hip ratio ( P = 0.045), and fasting insulin (P = 0.017) also signi ﬁcantly decreased. || The delta values (ﬁnal value minus baseline value of each variable) were not signi ﬁcantly different between groups, with the exception of ALT values (P = 0.02) (data not shown). || Moreover, the a diversity indicated that there were no differences, in terms of complexity (P > 0.05), between the dietary intervention (control versus probi- otic) at baseline as well as across time. || A signiﬁcant increase both in number of bacterial species (P = 0.01) and in bacterial diversity evaluated with the Chao1 index at T2 was observed in the treated participants but not in controls (Table 2). || / Nutrition 74 (2020) 110749 3 Anthropometric, metabolic, and lifestyle 