PMID:39664910,PubMed,39664910,Effect of synbiotic supplementation on obesity and gut microbiota in obese adults: a double-blind randomized controlled trial.,microbiome,"Body composition, blood lipids, serum hormone, bile acids, and gut microbiota were measured pre-and post-intervention.; Synbiotics supplementation significantly decreased body fat percentage, waist, and serum low-density lipoprotein cholesterol (LDL-C), increased peptide YY, cholecystokinin, oxyntomodulin, GSH (glutathione peroxidase) in individuals with obesity.",,,,,,,,80 individuals,detail_hint,expansion_queue,pending,,fnut-11-1510318.pdf,1.000,11,synbiotic supplementation on obesity and gut microbiota in obese adults; Bifidobacterium animalis subsp. lactis MN-Gup; synbiotic supplementation led to an enrichment of beneficial bacteria and bile acids chen; Bifidobacterium and Romboutsia were significantly positively correlated with CDCA. A more favor; inulin; polydextrose are commonly combined with probiotics to form synbiotics; Lactobacillus acidophilus; Bifidobacterium lactis,12 weeks; 4 weeks; 1 month; 3 months,"1 × 1011 CFU/day, GOS 0.7 g/da; 1 × 10 11 CFU/day, GOS 0.7 g/da",,microbiota; body fat; cholesterol; LDL; glucose; body mass index; BMI; Waist circumference; triglycerides; HDL,"Frontiers in Nutrition 01 frontiersin.org Effect of synbiotic supplementation on obesity and gut microbiota in obese adults: a double-blind randomized controlled trial Xiaokang Niu 1†, Qi Zhang 2†, Julong Liu 3†, Yuyang Zhao 1, Nan Shang 1, Shusen Li 3, Yinghua Liu 4, Wei Xiong 5, Erna Sun 3, Yong Zhang 4, Hongfeng Zhao 3, Yixuan Li 1, Pengjie Wang 1, Bing Fang 2, Liang Zhao 2, Juan Chen 1, Fuqing Wang 6, Guofang Pang 7, Chenyuan Wang 3*, Jingjing He 2* and Ran Wang 1* 1 Key Laboratory of Functional Dairy, Co-Constructed by Ministry of Education and Beijing Government, Department of Nutrition and Health, China Agricultural University, Beijing, China, 2 Research Center for Probiotics, China Agricultural University, Beijing, China, 3 Mengniu Hi-Tech Dairy Product Beijing Co., Ltd., Beijing, China, 4 Department of Nutrition, The First Medical Center of Chinese PLA General Hospital, Beijing,",Bifidobacterium animalis; Bifidobacterium and; Lactobacillus acidophilus; Bifidobacterium lactis; Bifidobacterium longum; Bifidobacterium bifidum; Bifidobacterium lactis UBBLa-70; Bifidobacterium in; Bifidobacterium has; Lactobacillus casei; Lactobacillus reuteri J1,,1011 CFU/day; 10 11 CFU/day,12 weeks; 4 weeks; 1 month; 3 months,39 subjects; 113 subjects; 80 subjects; 40 subjects; 67 subjects; n = 40; n = 35; n = 32; n = 9; n = 12; n = 14; n = 16,double-blind; randomized; controlled trial; placebo-controlled; Clinical trial; pilot; crossover,synbiotic; powder; yogurt; sachet; prebiotic; diet; supplement,p < 0.05; p > 0.05; p = 0.01; p = 0.001; p = 0.0001; p = 0.005; p = 0.03; p < 0.01; p < 0.001; p = 0.006; p = 0.0002; p = 0.02; p = 0.0006; p = 0.04; p = 0.05,"0.7 g/day; decreased body fat percentage, waist, and serum low-density lipoprotein cholesterol (LDL; increased peptide YY, cholecystokinin, oxyntomodulin, GSH (glutathione peroxidase) in in; reduced body fat percentage, waist, and serum LDL-C, especially in individuals with ob; changes in gut microbiota, bile acids and gut hormones; increase the survival rate of probiotics in the gastrointestinal tract ( 18); reduce weight in individuals with obesity ( 24); 28 kg/m2; 25%; 30%; change method (27); 50%; 75%; 100%; change of body fat percentage and 39 subjects per group were needed with an α-error of; decrease in BMI (p = 0; changes were observed in the placebo group; decrease in body fat percentage ( −0","Frontiers in Nutrition 01 frontiersin.org Effect of synbiotic supplementation on obesity and gut microbiota in obese adults: a double-blind randomized controlled trial Xiaokang Niu 1†, Qi Zhang 2†, Julong Liu 3†, Yuyang Zhao 1, Nan Shang 1, Shusen Li 3, Yinghua Liu 4, Wei Xiong 5, Erna Sun 3, Yong Zhang 4, Hongfeng Zhao 3, Yixuan Li 1, Pengjie Wang 1, Bing F || OPEN ACCESS EDITED BY Xiaolong Ji, Zhengzhou University of Light Industry, China REVIEWED BY Junhua Xie, Nanchang University, China Jiajia Song, Southwest University, China *CORRESPONDENCE Chenyuan Wang wangchenyuan@mengniu.cn Jingjing He hejingjing89@cau.edu.cn Ran Wang wangran@cau.edu.cn †These authors have contributed equally to this work Received 12 Octo || lactis MN-Gup, synbiotics, obesity, gut microbiota, randomized controlled trial 1 Introduction Obesity represents a serious global public health challenge, with its prevalence escalating rapidly over the past five decades, affecting approximately 700 million adults worldwide (1). || Probiotics have garnered significant attention in the application of relieving obesity due to their potential in regulating gut microbiota and its metabolites (3–7). || For instance, a combination of Lactobacillus acidophilus, Bifidobacterium lactis, Bifidobacterium longum, and Bifidobacterium bifidum and GOS was found to regulate gut microbiota and reduce weight in individuals with obesity ( 24). || Synbiotics containing Bifidobacterium lactis UBBLa-70 and FOS not only improved body composition but also regulated glucose and lipid metabolism ( 25).","Frontiers in Nutrition 01 frontiersin.org Effect of synbiotic supplementation on obesity and gut microbiota in obese adults: a double-blind randomized controlled trial Xiaokang Niu 1†, Qi Zhang 2†, Julong Liu 3†, Yuyang Zhao 1, Nan Shang 1, Shusen Li 3, Yinghua Liu 4, Wei Xiong 5, Erna Sun 3, Yong Zhang 4, Hongfeng Zhao 3, Yixuan Li 1, Pengjie Wang 1, Bing Fang 2, Liang Zhao 2, Juan Chen 1, Fuqing Wang 6, Guofang Pang 7, Chenyuan Wang 3*, Jingjing He 2* and Ran Wang 1* 1 Key Laboratory of Functional Dairy, Co-Constructed by Ministry of Education and Beijing Government, Department of Nutrition and Health, China Agricultural University, Beijing, China, 2 Research Center for Probiotics, China A",ok,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,fnut-11-1510318.pdf,1.000,11,"During the intervention period, everyone was instructed to consume a sachet of synbiotics (containing MN-Gup 1 × 10 11 CFU/day, GOS 0.7 g/day, and XOS 0.7 g/day) or a sachet of placebo (maltodextrin powder without probiotics and prebiotic) once daily after either lunch or dinner for a duration of 12 weeks. || Moreover, the synbiotic group showed significantly lower post-intervention-LDL-C levels compared to the placebo group (p = 0.05), with a greater reduction in LDL-C (p = 0.04). || However, the synbiotic group showed significantly higher levels of PYY (p = 0.006) and OXM (p = 0.0002) compared to the placebo group after intervention, with a particularly notable inter-group difference in the change of OXM (p = 0.02). || As for antioxidant indexes, after intervention, the serum GSH levels of both groups significantly increased (p < 0.01), but the GSH levels in the synbiotics group were significantly higher than those in the placebo group after intervention (p = 0.0001), and the increase value was significantly greater than that in the placebo group (p = 0.005). || 3.2 Effect of synbiotics intervention on body composition and serum indicators of individuals with obesity As demonstrated in Table 2, the synbiotic group exhibited a significant decrease in BMI (p = 0.01), body fat percentage (p = 0.001), waist ( p = 0.0001), and serum LDL-C level ( p = 0.005) after the intervention, while no significant changes were observed in the placebo group. || After the intervention, dietary intakes did not change in both groups compared TABLE 2 Effect of synbiotics intervention on body composition of individuals with obesity. || Beta diversity based on PCoA showed that the cluster of placebo group did not clearly separate from the cluster of the synbiotic group at baseline and post-intervention (Supplementary Figures S3E,F). || Frontiers in Nutrition 01 frontiersin.org Effect of synbiotic supplementation on obesity and gut microbiota in obese adults: a double-blind randomized controlled trial Xiaokang Niu 1†, Qi Zhang 2†, Julong Liu 3†, Yuyang Zhao 1, Nan Shang 1, Shusen Li 3, Yinghua Liu 4, Wei Xiong 5, Erna Sun 3, Yong Zhang 4, Hongfeng Zhao 3, Yixuan Li 1, Pengjie Wang 1, Bing Fang 2, Liang Zhao 2, Juan Chen 1, Fuqing Wang 6, Guofang Pang 7, Chenyuan Wang 3*, Jingjing He 2* and Ran Wang 1* 1 Key Laboratory of Functional Dairy, Co-Const","However, the synbiotic group showed significantly higher levels of PYY (p = 0.006) and OXM (p = 0.0002) compared to the placebo group after intervention, with a particularly notable inter-group difference in the change of OXM (p = 0.02). || At baseline, there were no statistically significant differences (p > 0.05) in age, sex, BMI, body fat percentage, waist circumference, hip circumference, soft lean mass between the two groups (Table 1). || As for antioxidant indexes, after intervention, the serum GSH levels of both groups significantly increased (p < 0.01), but the GSH levels in the synbiotics group were significantly higher than those in the placebo group after intervention (p = 0.0001), and the increase value was significantly greater than that in the placebo group (p = 0.005). || 3.2 Effect of synbiotics intervention on body composition and serum indicators of individuals with obesity As demonstrated in Table 2, the synbiotic group exhibited a significant decrease in BMI (p = 0.01), body fat percentage (p = 0.001), waist ( p = 0.0001), and serum LDL-C level ( p = 0.005) after the intervention, while no significant changes were observed in the placebo group. || Besides, there was a significant inter-group difference regarding the changes of LDL-C (-0.19 mmol/L vs. || p < 0.05 indicates a statistically significant difference. || Additionally, a significant increase in DCA was observed in the synbiotic group compared to the baseline (p < 0.05). || Moreover, the synbiotic group showed significantly lower post-intervention-LDL-C levels compared to the placebo group (p = 0.05), with a greater reduction in LDL-C (p = 0.04).","At baseline, there were no statistically significant differences (p > 0.05) in age, sex, BMI, body fat percentage, waist circumference, hip circumference, soft lean mass between the two groups (Table 1). || 3.2 Effect of synbiotics intervention on body composition and serum indicators of individuals with obesity As demonstrated in Table 2, the synbiotic group exhibited a significant decrease in BMI (p = 0.01), body fat percentage (p = 0.001), waist ( p = 0.0001), and serum LDL-C level ( p = 0.005) after the intervention, while no significant changes were observed in the placebo group. || Placebo group Synbiotic group V0 V1 Change p0 V0 V1 Change p0 p1 p2 BMI (kg/ m2) 26.84 (24.85, 30.72) 26.45 (24.79, 28.81) -0.11 ± 0.65 0.43 28.35 (24.25, 31.50) 28.05 (23.70, 31.20) -0.32 ± 0.75 0.01 0.51 0.17 Body fat percentage (%) 36.40 ± 4.12 36.04 ± 4.23 -0.36 ± 1.65 0.21 37.12 ± 6.89 35.86 ± 7.30 -1.26 ± 1.66 0.001 0.89 0.02 Waist (cm) 88.00 (79.00, 96.00) 86.00 (79.00, 93.00) -0.40 ± 4.44 0.56 89.50 (75.00, 98.50) 87.00 (73.50, 99.00) -2.28 ± 3.55 0.0001 0.78 0.02 Hip (cm) 102.33 ± 8.43 100.86 ± 9.01 -1.47 || Placebo group Synbiotic group Change p0 Change p0 p1 p2 Subgroup1 (n = 9) (n = 12) BMI (kg/m2) -0.14 ± 0.63 0.34 -0.25 ± 0.82 0.29 0.72 0.48 Body fat percentage (%) 0.19 ± 2.14 0.81 -1.46 ± 1.86 0.02 0.87 0.08 Waist (cm) 0.33 ± 3.24 0.82 -2.69 ± 4.29 0.07 0.87 0.09 Hip (cm) -1.33 ± 2.69 0.18 -3.69 ± 3.99 0.006 0.76 0.14 Soft lean mass (%) 0.87 ± 0.35 0.01 1.41 ± 1.74 0.01 0.99 0.56 TG (mmol/L) 0.22 ± 0.77 0.39 -0.17 ± 0.28 0.05 0.76 0.15 TC (mmol/L) 0.20 ± 0.60 0.31 -0.30 ± 0.64 0.12 0.16 0.07 LDL-C (mmol/L) 0.04 ± || Placebo (n = 40) Synbiotics (n = 40) p Age 34 (25, 38) 29 (22, 37) 0.21 Sex (male: female) (%) 27.50: 72.50 30.00: 70.00 1.00 BMI (kg/m2) 26.84 (24.85, 30.72) 28.35 (24.25, 31.50) 0.48 Body fat percentage (%) 36.40 ± 4.12 37.12 ± 6.89 0.70 Waist (cm) 88.00 (79.00, 96.00) 89.50 (75.00, 98.50) 0.73 Hip (cm) 102.33 ± 8.43 104.60 ± 8.72 0.32 Soft lean ma