seibacillus rhamnosus HA-114 improves eating behaviors and mood- related factors in adults with overweight during weight loss: a randomized controlled trial Béatrice S.-Y. || Objective: Our aim was to determine if Lacticaseibacillus rhamnosus HA-114 supplementation accentuated bene ﬁcial impact of weight loss on metabolic and cognitive health. || Methods: This 12-week randomized, double-blind, placebo-controlled trial assessed biological markers of energy metabolism, eating behaviors and mood-related factors in 152 adults with overweight receiving L. || rhamnosus HA-114 supplementation or placebo, that were also on a dietary intervention inducing a controlled weight loss.","Full Terms & Conditions of access and use can be found at https://www.tandfonline.com/action/journalInformation-journalCode=ynns20 Nutritional Neuroscience An International Journal on Nutrition, Diet and Nervous System ISSN: (Print) (Online) Journal homepage: https://www.tandfonline.com/loi/ynns20 Lacticaseibacillus rhamnosus HA-114 improves eating behaviors and mood-related factors in adults with overweight during weight loss: a randomized controlled trial Béatrice S.-Y. Choi, Lucie Brunelle, Geneviève Pilon, Brunella Gonzalez Cautela, Thomas A. Tompkins, Vicky Drapeau, André Marette & Angelo Tremblay To cite this article: Béatrice S.-Y. Choi, Lucie Brunelle, Geneviève Pilon, Brunella Gonza",ok,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,Lacticaseibacillus rhamnosus HA-114 improves eating behaviors and mood-related factors in adults with overweight during weight l(科研通-ablesci.com).pdf,0.890,14,"Furthermore, an increase in the component Strategic Dieting Behavior was present only in the probiotic group ( p = 0.05) and a signi ﬁcant group-by-time inter- action e ﬀect was observed in the factors Attitude to Self Regulation and Avoidance of Fattening Foods, which were increased in the probiotic group com- pared to the placebo group ( p < 0.05). || Dietary intervention consisted in a 12-week food plan, inducing a daily caloric restriction of 500 kcal, during the same period they consumed the probiotic formulation or the placebo. || Neither diet nor pro- biotic supplementation a ﬀected overall gastrointestinal symptoms, as shown by the absence of change on these variables before and after the intervention (Figure 4(E)). || Intervention The probiotic, Lacticaseibacillus rhamnosus HA-114, was given under the form of freeze-dried bacteria cap- sules (10 × 10 9 CFU per capsule), provided by Lallemand Health Solutions (LHS; || As poten- tial treatment, the concept of psychobiotics, initially deﬁned as ‘live bacteria (probiotics) which, when ingested, confer mental health beneﬁ ts through inter- actions with commensal gut bacteria ’ now including prebiotics [12] as well as live biotherapeutics, synbiotics, paraprobiotics, postbiotics, and fermented food [ 13] has emerged. || Eating behaviors When evaluating binge eating behavior, a decrease was observed in the probiotic group only ( Figure 3 (A)), with a group-by-time interaction signi ﬁcance (p < 0.01). || Microbiota stability and gastrointes- tinal tolerance in response to a high-protein diet with and without a prebiotic, probiotic, and synbiotic: a ran- domized, double-blind, placebo-controlled trial in older women. || In addition, there was a decrease over time in anxiety (trait), perceived stress and depression score in the probiotic group ( p < 0.05) although there was no group-by-time interaction sig- niﬁcant e ﬀect ( Figure 4(B– D)).","Although there was no signi ﬁcant group-by-time interaction, leptin decreased in both groups while ghre- lin signiﬁcantly increased in the probiotic group only ( p < 0.05). || Physical activity levels increased slightly by the end of the intervention ( p < 0.05), but to an equivalent extent in the two groups, without a change in subjects ’ activity classi ﬁcation. || Furthermore, an increase in the component Strategic Dieting Behavior was present only in the probiotic group ( p = 0.05) and a signi ﬁcant group-by-time inter- action e ﬀect was observed in the factors Attitude to Self Regulation and Avoidance of Fattening Foods, which were increased in the probiotic group com- pared to the placebo group ( p < 0.05). || When evaluating the subscales for the restraint factor, we observerd a signi ﬁcant increase in both ﬂexible and rigid restraint on the pla- cebo and the probiotic groups and there was a ten- dency ( p = 0.07) for a group-by-time interaction on the rigid restraint, being further increased in the pro- biotic group compared to the placebo ( Figure 3 (C)). || Data are represented as Mean ± SD, Within-group signi ﬁcance (*p ≤ 0.05, ** p < 0.01). || Data are rep- resented as Mean ± SD, Within group signi ﬁcance (* p ≤ 0.05, ** p < 0.01), group-by-time interaction signi ﬁcance ( #p ≤ 0.05, ##p < 0.01). || Cognitive restraint increased in both groups, but this e ﬀect was more pronounced in the probiotic group, with a signi ﬁcant group-by-time interaction ( p = 0.05). || Eating behaviors When evaluating binge eating behavior, a decrease was observed in the probiotic group only ( Figure 3 (A)), with a group-by-time interaction signi ﬁcance (p < 0.01).","Placebo Probiotic Baseline 3 months Baseline 3 months Body weight, kg 89.0 ± 13.3 (72) 84.8 ± 12.3** (63) 89.2 ± 12.1 (78) 84.2 ± 10.2** (69) Body Mass Index, kg/m 2 31.7 ± 2.8 (72) 30.3 ± 2.7** (63) 32.2 ± 3.1 (78) 30.7 ± 3.2** (69) Waist circumference, cm 96.6 ± 9.8 (72) 93.7 ± 9.3** (63) 96.3 ± 8.9 (78) 92.5 ± 8.3** (68) Fat mass, kg 35.9 ± 7.3 (72) 32.7 ± 7.0** (63) 37.0 ± 7.1 (77) 33.5 ± 7.7** (68) Lean mass, kg 48.8 ± 9.5 (72) 48.4 ± 9.7 (63) 47.8 ± 9.6 (77) 47.2 ± 9.0 (68) Body fat, % 41.1 ± 6.4 (72) 39.1 ± || Body weight and body composition The dietary intervention, aiming at a decrease of 500 kcal/day in energy intake, reduced body weight, body mass index, waist circumference, fat mass and percent body fat, whereas no signi ﬁcant change in lean mass was induced by the protocol ( Table 2). || Data are represented as Mean ± SD, Within-group signi ﬁcance (*p ≤ 0.05, ** p < 0.01). || Data are rep- resented as Mean ± SD, Within group signi ﬁcance (* p ≤ 0.05, ** p < 0.01), group-by-time interaction signi ﬁcance ( #p ≤ 0.05, ##p < 0.01). || Eating behaviors When evaluating binge eating behavior, a decrease was observed in the probiotic group only ( Figure 3 (A)), with a group-by-time interaction signi ﬁcance (p < 0.01). || Although on average the placebo group lost 2.96 kg of fat mass com- pared to 3.24 kg for the probiotic group, there was no signiﬁcant interaction with the probiotic supplemen- tation ( p > 0.05). || In addition, there was a decrease over time in anxiety (trait), perceived stress and depression score in the probiotic group ( p < 0.05) although there was no group-by-time interaction sig- niﬁcant e ﬀect ( Figure 4(B– D)). || The Three-Factor Eating Questionnaire (TFEQ) revealed a decrease in disinhibition and hun- ger in the probiotic group only ( p < 0.01), and a group-by-time interaction e ﬀect was also observed for these variables ( p = 0.05 and p < 0.01, respectively) (Figure 3(B)).","Placebo Probiotic Baseline 3 months Baseline 3 months Body weight, kg 89.0 ± 13.3 (72) 84.8 ± 12.3** (63) 89.2 ± 12.1 (78) 84.2 ± 10.2** (69) Body Mass Index, kg/m 2 31.7 ± 2.8 (72) 30.3 ± 2.7** (63) 32.2 ± 3.1 (78) 30.7 ± 3.2** (69) Waist circumference, cm 96.6 ± 9.8 (72) 93.7 ± 9.3** (63) 96.3 ± 8.9 (78) 92.5 ± 8.3** (68) Fat mass, kg 35.9 ± 7.3 (72) 32.7 ± 7.0** (63) 37.0 ± 7.1 (77) 33.5 ± 7.7** (68) Lean mass, kg 48.8 ± 9.5 (72) 48.4 ± 9.7 (63) 47.8 ± 9.6 (77) 47.2 ± 9.0 (68) Body fat, % 41.1 ± 6.4 (72) 39.1 ± || Interestingly, fasting plasma insulin, LDL-cholesterol and triglycerides levels as well as HOMA-IR were decreased over time within the probiotic group only (p < 0.05), although there was no signi ﬁcant group-by- time interaction. || The following blood variables were measured in the fasted state: glucose, insulin, total cholesterol, HDL- cholesterol, LDL-cholesterol, triglycerides, ALT and AST, C-reactive protein, estradiol, leptin and total ghre- lin. || As indicated above, a signi ﬁcant reduction in fasting levels of insulin, LDL-cholesterol, triglycerides and HOMA-IR was observed only in the probiotic group. || Results: Although probiotic supplementation did not potentiate the reduction in body weight or fat mass, a signi ﬁcant decrease in plasma insulin, HOMA-IR, LDL-cholesterol and triglycerides was observed in the probiotic-supplemented group only. || Glucose, total cholesterol, HDL-cholesterol, LDL- cholesterol, ALT and AST determinations were per- formed by a portable chemistry analyzer (Piccolo Xpress, Abaxis inc.); || As participants were healthy and did not su ﬀer from severe illnesses, including insulin-dependent diabetes, circulating levels of markers such as plasma NUTRITIONAL NEUROSCIENCE 5 triglycerides, cholesterol and c-reactive protein did not reach clinical diagnostic levels. || Data are represented as Mean ± SD, Within-group signi ﬁcance (*p ≤ 0.05, ** p < 0.01).","Data are represented as Mean ± SD, Within-group signi ﬁcance (*p ≤ 0.05, ** p < 0.01). || Data are rep- resented as Mean ± SD, Within group signi ﬁcance (* p ≤ 0.05, ** p < 0.01), group-by-time interaction signi ﬁcance ( #p ≤ 0.05, ##p < 0.01). || Eating behaviors When evaluating binge eating behavior, a decrease was observed in the probiotic group only ( Figure 3 (A)), with a group-by-time interaction signi ﬁcance (p < 0.01). || In addition, there was a decrease over time in anxiety (trait), perceived stress and depression score in the probiotic group ( p < 0.05) although there was no group-by-time interaction sig- niﬁcant e ﬀect ( Figure 4(B– D)). || The Three-Factor Eating Questionnaire (TFEQ) revealed a decrease in disinhibition and hun- ger in the probiotic group only ( p < 0.01), and a group-by-time interaction e ﬀect was also observed for these variables ( p = 0.05 and p < 0.01, respectively) (Figure 3(B)). || Furthermore, an increase in the component Strategic Dieting Behavior was present only in the probiotic group ( p = 0.05) and a signi ﬁcant group-by-time inter- action e ﬀect was observed in the factors Attitude to Self Regulation and Avoidance of Fattening Foods, which were increased in the probiotic group com- pared to the placebo group ( p < 0.05). || When evaluating the subscales for the restraint factor, we observerd a signi ﬁcant increase in both ﬂexible and rigid restraint on the pla- cebo and the probiotic groups and there was a ten- dency ( p = 0.07) for a group-by-time interaction on the rigid restraint, being further increased in the pro- biotic group compared to the placebo ( Figure 3 (C)). || Placebo Probiotic Baseline 3 months Baseline 3 months Body weight, kg 89.0 ± 13.3 (72) 84.8 ± 12.3** (63) 89.2 ± 12.1 (78) 84.2 ± 10.2** (69) Body Mass Index, kg/m 2 31.7 ± 2.8 (72) 30.3 ± 2.7** (63) 32.2 ± 3.1 (78) 30.7 ± 3.2** (69) Waist circumference, cm 96.6 ± 9.8 (72) 93.7 ± 9.3** (63) 96.3 ± 8.9 (78) 92.5 ± 8.3** (68) Fat mass, kg 35.9 ± 7.3 (72) 32.7 ± 7.0** (63) 37.0 ± 7.1 (77) 33.5 ± 7.7** (68) Lean mass, kg 48.8 ± 9.5 (72) 48.4 ± 9.7 (63) 47.8 ± 9.6 (77) 47.2 ± 9.0 (68) Body fat, % 41.1 ± 6.4 (72) 39.1 ±","Data are represented as Mean ± SD, Within-group signi ﬁcance (*p ≤ 0.05, ** p < 0.01). || Data are rep- resented as Mean ± SD, Within group signi ﬁcance (* p ≤ 0.05, ** p < 0.01), group-by-time interaction signi ﬁcance ( #p ≤ 0.05, ##p < 0.01). || Eating behaviors When evaluating binge eating behavior, a decrease was observed in the probiotic group only ( Figure 3 (A)), with a group-by-time interaction signi ﬁcance (p < 0.01). || In addition, there was a decrease over time in anxiety (trait), perceived stress and depression score in the probiotic group ( p < 0.05) although there was no group-by-time interaction sig- niﬁcant e ﬀect ( Figure 4(B– D)). || The Three-Factor Eating Questionnaire (TFEQ) revealed a decrease in disinhibition and hun- ger in the probiotic group only ( p 