ay βdiversity(P =0.639).Nosignificant differences in dispersion were observed between any groupandtimepointcombination.","Control (n = 12) Intervention ( n = 18) Pre Post Change Pre Post Change ANCOVA P-value Body mass (kg)2 86.7 (14.8) 87.2 (15.0) 0.57 (0.84) 91.0 (14.0) 88.4 (14.3) -2.6 (1.5) <0.001 Fat mass (kg)2 34.0 (9.8) 34.2 (9.9) 0.2 (1.0) 34.3 (5.7) 33.0 (5.9) -1.5 (1.3) 0.00100 Fat-free mass (kg)2 49.1 (9.8) 49.3 (10.2) 0.2 (0.9) 53.9 (11.6) 52.0 (12.2) -1.0 (1.9) 0.0430 Waist circumference (cm) 99 (13) 101 (10) 2 (3) 105 (9) 101 (10) -4 (3) 0.00200 Insulin (pmol/l) 62.3 (54.0) 66.5 (73.0) 4.2 (24.1) 69.5 (51.7) 46.0 (25.0) || Fasting plasma glucose, HDL cholesterol, CRP and LBP were not significantly differ en ta tfollo w-u p(T able2). || Davies and others J Physiol 603.24 increased insulin sensitivity and reduced fasting insulin, total and LDL cholesterol and leptin in the intervention group. || Fasted blood measurements Fasted serum insulin and plasma leptin, total cholesterol and low-density lipoprotein (LDL) cholesterol were all significantly lower in the intervention group at follow-up compared to control (Table 2). || The intervention significantly reduced body mass (mean/Delta1± SD: –2.6 ± 1.5 kg), fat mass (–1.5± 1.3 kg), fasted insulin (–23.5± 38.1 pmol/l), leptin (–10.6± 7.3 ng/ml) and total cholesterol (–0.70± 0.42 mmol/l) concentrations, and also improved insulin sensitivity (HOMA2%S (homeostatic model of assessment)). || Expression of all detected targeted genes in skeletal muscle was not significantly different between baseline and follow-up (LBPP = 0.256, CD14 P =0.529;Fig.9). || Plasma glucose, total cholesterol and high-density lipoprotein (HDL) cholesterol concentrations were analysed using commercially available kits on a Daytona-automated clinical chemistry analyser (Randox, Crumlin, UK). || It was estimated that a sample size of 24 (16 in intervention and 8 in control) would provide approximately 90% power to detect a reduction in fasting plasma insulin of –19.9 ± 1 8 .","OA articles are governed by the applicable Creative Commons License J Physiol 603.24 Energy restriction plus exercise and the human gut microbiome: an RCT 7741 Adipose and skeletal muscle gene expression We focused our attention on measuring the mRNA expression of genes coding for proteins reported to be importantformediatingtheeffectsofmicrobiotaonperi- pheral tissues: LBP and CD14 as potential mediators of the LPS effects, and FFAR2/GPR43 and FFAR3/GPR41 as potential mediators of the effects of SCFA. || Expression of all detected targeted genes in skeletal muscle was not significantly different between baseline and follow-up (LBPP = 0.256, CD14 P =0.529;Fig.9). || SCFAshavebeenimplicatedasoneofthelinksbetween the gut microbiome and metabolic health (Canfora et al., 2015, 2019). || Thepotentialmechanismswherebyexerciseandweight loss may exert positive effects on insulin sensitivity via the gut microbiome include the increased production of short-chain fatty acids (SCFAs) and activation of G-protein coupled receptors (GPR) 41 and GPR43 (also known as free fatty-acid receptors (FFARs) FFAR3 and FFAR2) in the peripheral tissues responsible for the disposal of ingested nutrients, such as skeletal muscle and subcutaneous adipose tissue (Canfora et al., 2015). || LBP and CD14/Delta1/Delta1Ct values in adipose tissue were not significantly different between groups (P = 0.606 and P =0.469,respectively;Fig.9). || No significant effect of group was observed on species β diversity (P = 0.101), nor on metabolicpathway βdiversity(P =0.639).Nosignificant differences in dispersion were observed between any groupandtimepointcombination. || In adipose tissue FFAR2/GPR43 was detected in n = 11 and 7 participants from control and intervention, r e s p e c t i v e l y ,b u tc h a n g e sw e r en o ts i g n i fi c a n t l yd i ff e r e n t between groups (P = 0.381). || Furthermore, there was no evidence of significant microbiome changes at the fu