, children who consumed OI had signi ﬁcant decreases in body weight z-score (decrease of 3.1%), percent body fat (decrease of 2.4%), and percent trunk fat (decrease of 3.8%) compared with children given placebo (increase of 0.5%, increase of 0.05%, and decrease of 0.3%, respectively).","Prebiotics Reduce Body Fat and Alter Intestinal Microbiota in Children Who Are Overweight or With Obesity Alissa C. Nicolucci, 1 Megan P. Hume, 1 Inés Martínez, 2 Shyamchand Mayengbam, 1 Jens Walter, 2,3 and Raylene A. Reimer 1,4 1Faculty of Kinesiology, University of Calgary, Calgary, Alberta, Canada; 2Department of Agricultural, Food and Nutritional Science, University of Alberta, Edmonton, Alberta, Canada; 3Department of Biological Sciences, University of Alberta, Edmonton, Alberta, Canada; 4Department of Biochemistry and Molecular Biology, University of Calgary, Calgary, Alberta, Canada BACKGROUND & AIMS: It might be possible to manipulate the intestinal microbiota with prebiotics or oth",ok,,PIIS0016508517356986.pdf,1.000,12,"9,10 These promising outcomes in adults justify the assessment of prebiotics as a dietary intervention to modulate gut microbiota and metabolic outcomes in pedi- atric obesity. || Prebiotics Induced Marginal Changes in Systemic In ﬂammation There was little change in the serum in ﬂammatory proﬁle within the prebiotic and placebo groups (Supplementary Table 1 ). || 30 Based on our 4-month intervention data, the annual projected body weight increase in the prebiotic group would be 3 kg, within the expected range, whereas the projected increase in the placebo group was 8 kg, almost triple the expected yearly increase. || The interac- tion of treatment and time signi ﬁcantly in ﬂuenced BMI (P ¼ .009) whereby there was no change in BMI within the prebiotic group compared with baseline, but at all 4 time points compared with baseline, BMI signi ﬁcantly increased in the placebo group ( Supplementary Figure 2 ). || Exclusion criteria included type 1 or 2 diabetes, 