PMID:40814108,PubMed,40814108,Postbiotics as a novel intervention for obesity management and improving metabolic parameters: a systematic review and meta-analysis of animal studies.,weight,"Therefore, the present study aimed to systematically assess the effect of postbiotics consumption on weight management, dietary intake, and metabolic indices in animal models of obesity.; The search was conducted using the PubMed, Scopus, Web of Science, and Google Scholar databases for relevant articles published in English up to May 2025.",P = 0.001; P = 0.011; P = 0.497,,,,,,,,detail_hint,expansion_queue,pending,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,s12967-025-06927-z.pdf,1.000,19,"We found 19 articles about HDL-C and combin - ing their results showed a significant reduction in HDL-C levels after postbiotic supplementation (WMD: 6.013 mg/dl, (95% CI 1.400 to 10.626), P = 0.011)) with Fig. || Pooled analysis showed a significant reduction in FBG levels following postbiotic supplementation (WMD: - 15.712 mg/dL, (95% CI - 23.651 to - 7.772), P < 0.001) with high heterogeneity (I 2 = 92.3% Pheterogeneity < 0.001)) (Fig. || The pooled effects of four studies that evaluated the effect of postbiotics on HOMA-IR measurement showed a notable decrease in HOMA-IR after postbiot - ics supplementation (WMD: - 11.040, (95% CI - 19.855 to - 2.225), P = 0.014) with a high heterogeneity among the studies (I 2 = 99.4% P heterogeneity < 0.001)) (Fig. || Accordingly, supplementation with different forms of postbiotics was accompanied by a significant decrease in the body weight, body fat, and serum levels of LDL-C, TG, TC, FBG, insulin, and HOMA-IR (P = 0.001, 0.040, < 0.001, < 0.001, < 0.001, < 0.001, < 0.001, 0.014 respectively) and significant increase of serum HDL-C level (P = 0.011). || Upon the subgroup analyses, SCFAs as a postbiotic supplement, the treatment dose of ≥ 100 mg/kg, and intervention duration ≥ 8 weeks were more effective in improving body weight and fat mass and FBG, HOMA-IR, and HDL-C levels; || Journal of Translational Medicine (2025) 23:913 studies with gavage feeding (I 2 = 0.0%), intraperitoneal injection (I 2 = 0.0%), postbiotics types of SCFAs (I 2 = 0%) and bacterial components (I 2 = 89.1%), and intervention dose ≥ 100 mg/kg (I 2 = 0.0%) (Supplementary Table 3). || Moreover, the results of the sub-group analysis demon - strated that postbiotics intake had significant effect on reducing LDL-C levels in the studies on C57BL/6J mice (I2 = 91.2%), oral fed (I 2 = 88.9%), postbiotics type of heat-killed probiotics (I 2 = 0%), and intervention dura - tion ≥ 8 weeks (I2 = 99.5%) (Supplementary Table 3). || Journal of Translational Medicine (2025) 23:913 that postbiotic intake significantly reduced serum FBG levels in the studies with sample size ≥ 40 (I2 = 0.0%), ani- mal age < 6 weeks (I 2 = 81.0%), oral feeding (I 2 = 69.8%), postbiotics type of SCFAs (I 2 = 82.5%), intervention dose ≥ 100 mg/kg (I 2 = 92.3%), and treatment dura - tion ≥ 8 weeks (I2 = 82.1%) (Supplementary Table 3).","However, no significant change was observed in food intake (P = 0.497). || Pooling their results showed a significant decrease in body weight (WMD: - 9.650 (95% CI - 15.259 to - 4.042), P = 0.001)), albeit with high heterogeneity (I 2 = 99.9%, Pheterogeneity < 0.001) (Fig. || We found 19 articles about HDL-C and combin - ing their results showed a significant reduction in HDL-C levels after postbiotic supplementation (WMD: 6.013 mg/dl, (95% CI 1.400 to 10.626), P = 0.011)) with Fig. || The combined results showed a significant decrease in body fat percentage (WMD: - 4.550 (95% CI - 8.895 to - 0.202), P = 0.040) after the intervention with a high heterogeneity (I 2 = 98.8% Pheterogeneity < 0.001) (Fig. || Eatheen studies showed a significant reduction in insulin levels after postbiotic administration (WMD: - 0.096 ng/ml (95% CI - 0.149 to - 0.043), P < 0.001) with high heterogeneity (I 2 = 94.1% Pheterogeneity < 0.001)) (Fig. || Pooled analysis showed a significant reduction in FBG levels following postbiotic supplementation (WMD: - 15.712 mg/dL, (95% CI - 23.651 to - 7.772), P < 0.001) with high heterogeneity (I 2 = 92.3% Pheterogeneity < 0.001)) (Fig. || The pooled results of these studies showed no significant impact on food intake following postbiotic intervention (WMD: - 0.036 (95% CI - 0.140 to 0.068), P = 0.497), with low heterogeneity (I2 = 0.0%, Pheterogeneity = 0.991) (Fig. || Based on the combined results of 25 articles, postbiot - ics supplementation had significant effects on decreas - ing serum TC levels (WMD: - 37.501 mg/dL, (95% CI - 54.621 to - 20.381), P < 0.001) with high heterogene - ity among the studies (I 2 = 99.0%, P heterogeneity < 0.001) (Fig.","Accordingly, supplementation with different forms of postbiotics was accompanied by a significant decrease in the body weight, body fat, and serum levels of LDL-C, TG, TC, FBG, insulin, and HOMA-IR (P = 0.001, 0.040, < 0.001, < 0.001, < 0.001, < 0.001, < 0.001, 0.014 respectively) and significant increase of serum HDL-C level (P = 0.011). || Pooling their results showed a significant decrease in body weight (WMD: - 9.650 (95% CI - 15.259 to - 4.042), P = 0.001)), albeit with high heterogeneity (I 2 = 99.9%, Pheterogeneity < 0.001) (Fig. || The combined results showed a significant decrease in body fat percentage (WMD: - 4.550 (95% CI - 8.895 to - 0.202), P = 0.040) after the intervention with a high heterogeneity (I 2 = 98.8% Pheterogeneity < 0.001) (Fig. || Journal of Translational Medicine (2025) 23:913 supplementation with different postbiotics results in a 9g decrease in body weight and a 4.5% reduction in body fat of obese animal models. || demonstrated that 6 weeks of supplementation with 10 9 CFU/day of heat- inactivated Lactobacillus rhamnosus GG in obese Wistar rats resulted in a significant reduction in visceral fat mass but not in food intake [36]. || Journal of Translational Medicine (2025) 23:913 Terms] OR “Extracellular Vesicles” [Titl