registered 16 May 2023)). || HOMA-IR decreased in the catechin + inulin group and increased in the placebo group, yielding a significant between-group difference (mean change: −0.32 vs. || Changes in Coprococcus were inversely correlated with changes in HOMA-IR (r =−0.28; || Conclusions: Catechin plus inulin for12 weeks Nutrients 2026, 18, 851 https://doi.org/10.3390/nu18050851 Nutrients 2026, 18, 851 2 of 15 did not reduce visceral fat but was associated with improved insulin resistance. || Introduction Visceral fat accumulation is strongly implicated in the development and progression of insulin resistance, type 2 diabetes, hypertension, dyslipidemia, and other obesity-related complications.","Academic Editors: Alina Kuryłowicz and Monika Stołyhwo-Gofron Received: 28 January 2026 Revised: 26 February 2026 Accepted: 3 March 2026 Published: 6 March 2026 Copyright: © 2026 by the authors. Licensee MDPI, Basel, Switzerland. This article is an open access article distributed under the terms and conditions of the Creative Commons Attribution (CC BY) license. Article Green Tea Catechin Plus Inulin Improves Insulin Resistance Without Reducing Visceral Fat and Shows Exploratory Gut Microbiota Signals in Adults with Visceral Obesity: A Double-Blind Randomized Controlled Trial Chikara Iino 1,* , Keita Mikami 1, Keisuke Furusawa 1, Satoshi Sato 1 , Kenta Yoshida 1 , Tohru Yamaguchi2,3 , Hiroto",ok,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,nutrients-18-00851.pdf,1.000,15,"The between-group difference in VFA change was not statistically significant (p = 0.243), indicating that the intervention did not produce a clear reduction in VFA compared with placebo under the present conditions (Figure 2). || Previous studies have reported that lower abun- dances of butyrate-producing bacteria and reduced SCFA levels are associated with obesity, insulin resistance, and metabolic syndrome [10,11], whereas dietary fiber interventions that increase SCFA production can ameliorate these conditions [8–10]. || Reduced SCFA concentrations and depletion of butyrate-producing bacte- ria have been observed in obesity and type 2 diabetes [10,11], whereas dietary interventions with fermentable fibers that increase SCFA production are often associated with improve- ments in insulin resistance and inflammatory markers [ 8–10]. || Second, while the catechin dose in this study (400 mg/day) falls within the lower range of doses used in earlier trials, several positive studies employed higher doses of catechins (e.g., 500–600 mg/day or more) and sometimes combined catechin intake with additional lifestyle interventions such as caloric restriction or exercise [3–5]. || +0.18 ± 0.76), with a statistically significant between-group difference (p = 0.020). || In contrast, the change in Bifidobacterium relative abundance was not clearly associated with the change in HOMA-IR (r = 0, p = 0.95). || CAP , an index of liver fat, changed by-2.0 ± 58.5 dB/m (n = 44) in thecatechin + inulin group and by -6.8 ± 64.1 dB/m (n = 42) in the placebo group over 12 weeks, with no significant between-group difference (p = 0.72). || The change in Coprococcus relative abundance from baseline to week 12 was inversely correlated with the change in HOMA-IR (r = -0.28, p = 0.010), indicating that participants with greater increases in Coprococcus tended to exhibit greater improvements in HOMA-IR (Figure 3).","HOMA-IR decreased in the catechin + inulin group and increased in the placebo group, yielding a significant between-group difference (mean change: -0.32 vs. || CAP , an index of liver fat, changed by-2.0 ± 58.5 dB/m (n = 44) in thecatechin + inulin group and by -6.8 ± 64.1 dB/m (n = 42) in the placebo group over 12 weeks, with no significant between-group difference (p = 0.72). || The between-group difference in VFA change was not statistically significant (p = 0.243), indicating that the intervention did not produce a clear reduction in VFA compared with placebo under the present conditions (Figure 2). || +0.18 ± 0.76), with a statistically significant between-group difference (p = 0.020). || From baseline to week 12, HOMA-IR decreased in the catechin + inulin group and increased in the placebo group (mean change: -0.32 ± 1.71 vs. || In contrast, the change in Bifidobacterium relative abundance was not clearly associated with the change in HOMA-IR (r = 0, p = 0.95). || AST and ALT increased modestly in the catechin + inulin group, whereas they remained relatively stable in the placebo group, resulting in statistically significant between-group differences. || The change in Coprococcus relative abundance from baseline to week 12 was inversely correlated with the change in HOMA-IR (r = -0.28, p = 0.010), indicating that participants with greater increases in Coprococcus tended to exhibit greater improvements in HOMA-IR (Figure 3).","Supplementation with inulin or inulin-type fructans (ITFs) has been reported to selectively increase beneficial bacteria such as Bifidobac- terium spp., enhance the production of short-chain fatty acids (SCFAs), and thereby reduce body weight, BMI, body fat mass, and waist circumference, as well as improve fasting glucose, HbA1c, and HOMA-IR levels in several RCTs and meta-analyses [8,9]. || Box- and-whisker plots of 12-week changes in visceral fat area (VFA), body weight, BMI, and waist circumference by group. || The two groups were generally well balanced at baseline with respect to age, sex distribution, VFA, body weight, BMI, waist circumference, SBP , DBP , fasting glucose, HbA1c, lipid profile (TG, HDL-C, and LDL-C), and liver enzymes (AST, ALT, and GGT). || Variable Catechin + Inulin (n = 47) Median (IQR) Placebo (n = 44) Median (IQR) p Value Sex: female 1 45% (21) 43% (19) 1 2 Age (years) 57.0 (44.5, 64.5) 56.5 (45.8, 66.0) 0.947 Visceral fat area, VFA (cm2) 108.7 (93.7, 138.7) 112.8 (94.8, 140.1) 0.780 Body weight (kg) 70.0 (65.4, 77.1) 70.1 (65.0, 76.1) 0.495 BMI (kg/m2) 26.9 (25.2, 28.5) 26.1 (24.4, 27.9) 0.379 Waist circumference (cm) 91.4 