PMID:40375569,PubMed,40375569,"Mental health and health-related quality of life response to curcumin-probiotic drink powder co-supplementation in adults with overweight/obesity and metabolic syndrome: a four-arm randomised, double-blind, placebo-controlled, clinical trial.",weight,Curcumin-probiotics co-supplementation could improve the mental health and HRQOL of adults with overweight/obesity and MetS.,,,,,,,,104 participants,detail_hint,expansion2_queue,pending,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,mental-health-and-health-related-quality-of-life-response-to-curcumin-probiotic-drink-powder-co-supplementation-in-adults-with-overweightobesity-and-metabolic-syndrome-a-four-arm-randomised-double-bli.pdf,1.000,11,"Participants were randomly allocated into four groups to receive one drink powder sachet containing 1 g curcumin, 10 9 colony-forming unit (CFU) probiotic (Lactobacillus acidophilus and Lactobacillus rhamnosus strains), CurPro (1 g curcumin and 109 CFU probiotic) or placebo along with a low-calorie diet. || An 8-week probiotics supplementation was observed to improve mental health (41). || On the other hand, probiotic supplements were observed to increase quality of life (53,54). || Ale EC & Binetti AG (2021) Role of probiotics, prebiotics, and synbiotics in the elderly: insights into their applications. || (2021) The effect of synbiotic and probiotic supplementation on mental health parameters in patients undergoing hemodialysis: a double-blind, randomized, placebo- controlled trial. || Low adherence to the intervention was defined as consumption of less than 80 % of sachets based on the counting of returned unused sachets at weeks 4 and 8, or low compliance with diet, considering less than 50 % of calorie reduction during the study period in comparison with the pre-intervention diet records. || Analysed (n 26) Excluded from the final analysis (n 4) Non-adherence to diet (n 1) Non-adherence to drinks (n 2) Used curcumin supplement (n 1) Lost to follow-up (n 2) Car accident (n 1) Not responded to telephone calls (n 1) Follow-Up Allocated to placebo (n 32) Allocation Assessed for eligibility (n 862) Excluded (n 734) Not meeting inclusion criteria (n 317) Declined to participate (n 152) Other reasons (n 265) Randomised (n 128) Enrollment Allocated to probiotic (n 32) Allocated to CurPro (n 32) Allocated to cu || Dietary intake and physical activity changes of participants during the study period based on the study groups Placebo (n 26) Probiotic (n 26) Curcumin (n 26) CurPro (n 26) P- value*Mean SD Mean SD Mean SD Mean SD Energy (kcal/day) –656·64 671 ·75 –467·72 288 ·89 –471·24 356 ·79 –555·54 454 ·76 0 ·425 Protein (g/day) –23·89 26 ·58 –20·53 21 ·76 –14·41 19 ·60 –15·33 16 ·30 0 ·338 Carbohydrate (g/day) –93·23 100 ·61 –76·24 54 ·52 –69·76 74 ·83 –84·69 81 ·66 0 ·734 Fat (g/day) –22·06 34 ·13 –10·14 15 ·61 –14·35 25 ·47","Statistical analysis showed significant differences in changes in stress scores between groups ( P = 0·001). || Among HRQOL subdomains ’ scores, between-group compar- isons for changes in physical functioning, vitality, social function- ing, RE and mental health scores did not show any significant differences. || During the study, participants’ stress scores increased in the placebo group (0·15 ± 0·54), this is while scores decreased in the probiotic (–2·96 ± 4·83, P Placebo v. || Anxiety score increased in the Placebo group (0 ·69 ± 0·27) and decreased in Probiotics (–2·23 ± 4·46, PPlacebo v.P r o b i o t i c= 0·049), curcumin ( –1·53 ± 2·84 PPlacebo v. || The CurPro intervention reduced stress (P = 0·001) and anxiety (P = 0·019) and improved general health ( P = 0·024) and overall HRQOL (P = 0·011) scores of participants in comparison with the Placebo group. || As positive results were observed in the Probiotic group for depression, non- significant results could be possibly due to the low dosage of curcumin (1 g per day or lower) (47) and the short duration of the study. || Post hoc analyses showed differences for RP score ( P = 0·040), general health score ( P = 0·017) and total score ( P = 0·007) of HRQOL between Placebo and CurPro groups, while for bodily pain score, a difference was seen between Placebo and Curcumin groups (P = 0·49). || This is while changes in RP ( P = 0·034), bodily pain (P = 0·0·38), general health (P = 0·024) scores and the total score of British Journal of Nutrition 1323 https://doi.org/10.1017/S0007114525000923 Published online by Cambridge University Press SF-36 (P = 0·011) were significantly different between groups.","Demographic and baseline characteristics of participants based on the groups Placebo (n 26) Probiotic (n 26) Curcumin (n 26) CurPro (n 26) n or mean % or SD n or mean % or SD n or mean % or SD n or mean % or SD Gender n (%) Male 4 15 ·42 7 ·74 1 5 ·47 2 6 ·9 Female 22 84 ·62 4 9 2 ·32 2 8 4 ·61 9 7 3 ·1 Marital Status n (%) Single 2 7 ·73 1 1 ·52 7 ·73 1 1 ·5 Married 24 92 ·32 3 8 8 ·52 4 9 2 ·32 3 8 8 ·5 Education level n (%) High school or lower 10 38 ·51 4 5 3 ·81 2 4 6 ·21 0 3 8 ·5 Diploma or higher 16 61 ·51 2 || An 8-week probiotics supplementation was observed to improve mental health (41). || On the other hand, probiotic supplements were observed to increase quality of life (53,54). || It is proposed that probiotics could reduce constipation, increase fecal short-chain fatty acids, Table 2. || A bioelectrical impedance analysis device (Tanita, model: BC-418, Japan) was used to measure weight and BMI. || However, participants in the CurPro group had lower BMI in comparison to the other groups ( P = 0·005) ( Table 1 ). || Demographic and baseline characteristics of participants Table 1 shows the demographic and baseline characteristics of the participants. || Finally, 104 participants (17/87, male/female) entered the final analysis (twenty-six in each group) with a mean age of 48 ·98 ± 7·27 (mean ± SD).","Iran) and HDL- cholesterol (commercial kit: BXC0420A, Biorex Inc. || (2021) The effects of flaxseed supplem