ised controlled trial, Clinical Nutrition ESPEN, https:// doi.org/10.1016/j.clnesp.2019.10.001 of weight ( P ¼ 0.005) in the intervention group. || / Clinical Nutrition ESPEN xxx (xxxx) xxx6 Please cite this article as: Razmpoosh E et al., Effect of a low energy diet, containing a high protein, probiotic condensed yogurt, on biochemical and anthropometric measurements among women with overweight/obesity: A randomised controlled trial, Clinical Nutrition ESPEN, https:// doi.org/10.1016/j.clnesp.2019.10.001 and include more intervention and/or placebo groups to compare the individual or synergistic effects of Kashk and low energy diet. || found that, lifestyle intervention for 6 months in obese elderly adults led to a decrease in weight changes as well as sig- niﬁcant decreases in the measures of WC, FPG, serum TG, SBP and DBP levels (all P < 0.05) [ 36]. || used a 6-week inter- vention of 300 g/d of probiotic yogurt containing the same colony counts of L. || The intervention group was instructed to consume a daily dose of 50 g of Kashk with their weight loss diet for 8 weeks. || / Clinical Nutrition ESPEN xxx (xxxx) xxx 7 Please cite this article as: Razmpoosh E et al., Effect of a low energy diet, containing a high protein, probiotic condensed yogurt, on biochemical and anthropometric measurements among women with overweight/obesity: A randomised controlled trial, Clinical Nutrition ESPEN, https:// doi.org/10.1016/j.clnesp.2019.10.001 || / Clinical Nutrition ESPEN xxx (xxxx) xxx2 Please cite this article as: Razmpoosh E et al., Effect of a low energy diet, containing a high protein, probiotic condensed yogurt, on biochemical and anthropometric measurements among women with overweight/obesity: A randomised controlled trial, Clinical Nutrition ESPEN, https:// doi.org/10.1016/j.clnesp.2019.10.001 requirement of every participant.","found that, lifestyle intervention for 6 months in obese elderly adults led to a decrease in weight changes as well as sig- niﬁcant decreases in the measures of WC, FPG, serum TG, SBP and DBP levels (all P < 0.05) [ 36]. || Between-group comparisons revealed that there were signi ﬁcant differences in the mean changes of BMI (P ¼ 0.018), body fat percent ( P ¼ 0.037) and WC ( P ¼ 0.047) after the intervention period, all of which found to be signi ﬁcantly lower in intervention group compared to the control. || Differences with P < 0.05 were considered to be statistically signi ﬁcant. || After the intervention, within-group comparisons showed that the energy, fat and carbohydrate intakes were signi ﬁcantly reduced among both intervention and control groups (all P < 0.01). || Further, the consumption of ﬁber was signiﬁcantly increased in the control group in within-group differences ( P ¼ 0.005). || Results: Between-group comparisons showed that the intervention group signi ﬁcantly decreased triglycerides (P ¼ 0.013) and low density lipoprotein cholesterol ( P ¼ 0.049) levels relative to the control group. || In contrast, the comparisons between the two groups showed no signi ﬁcant differences in the levels of the dietary intakes after the intervention, in spite of lower but not signiﬁcant changes of fat intakes (P ¼ 0.05). || After an eight-week intervention period, between-group alterations reached signi ﬁcant results for the measures of LDL-C ( P ¼ 0.049) and TG ( P ¼ 0.013), which found to be signi ﬁcantly lower among intervention group compared to the control.","Property Group Before Study (Mean ± SD) After Study (Mean ± SD) Change (Mean ± SD) P Value a Weight (kg) Intervention 81.5 ± 12.0 77.5 ± 12.0 /C0 4 ± 2.5 0.005* Control 77.0 ± 14.0 73.0 ± 16.0 /C0 4.0 ± 9.0 0.12 P Value b 0.15 0.8 BMI (kg/m 2) Intervention 32.0 ± 4.0 31.0 ± 4.0 /C0 1.5 ± 1.0 0.005* Control 31.0 ± 5.11 29.64 ± 5.14 /C0 0.94 ± 0.75 0.005* P Value b 0.19 0.018* Body fat mass (kg) Intervention 33.0 ± 8.0 29.5 ± 8.5 /C0 3.5 ± 2 0.005* Control 30.0 ± 10.0 27.5 ± 10.5 /C0 2.5 ± 2 0.005* P Value b 0.16 0.0 || Dairy products have been